{"k": "234cd9af71f573ebab2ef7b5300cdd67", "t": "\n\"American Pie\" is a classic song written and recorded by Don McLean in 1971. The lyrics are poetic and open to interpretation, but here is one possible set of lyrics:\n\nVerse 1:\nA long, long time ago, I can still remember How that music used to make me smile And I knew if I had my chance That I could make those people dance And maybe they'd be happy for a while But February made me shiver With every paper I'd deliver Bad news on the doorstep And though it's hard to believe Now I know I'm not alone In this vast unknown\n\nChorus:\nOh, and while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home And he wrote in his diary With great fanfare And he said \"This could be the start Of something unreal\"\n\nVerse 2:\nNow for ten years we've been on our own And moss grows fat on a rolling stone But that's not how it used to be When the jester sang for the king and queen In a coat he borrowed from James Dean And a voice that came from you know where Oh, and while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home\n\nChorus:\nOh, and while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home And he wrote in his diary With great fanfare And he said \"This could be the start Of something unreal\"\n\nBridge:\nAnd while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home He saw a little silhouette Of a man with long blonde hair And he said \"I'm not too sure if I know him From someplace in my past or if he's just a figment of my imagination\"\n\nChorus:\nOh, and while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home And he wrote in his diary With great fanfare And he said \"This could be the start Of something unreal\"\n\nOutro:\nAnd now for ten years we've been on our own But the kid is grown, the jester's gone And I'm older than I was When I first saw that light In the window of my cell And I know if I"}
{"k": "ba64b133bfb7f5eed06d58e72a19d469", "t": "\n\"American Pie\" is a classic song written and recorded by Don McLean in 1971. The lyrics are poetic and open to interpretation, but here is one possible set of lyrics:\n\nVerse 1:\nA long, long time ago, I can still remember How that music used to make me smile And I knew if I had my chance That I could make those people dance And maybe they'd be happy for a while But February made me shiver With every paper I'd deliver Bad news on the doorstep And though it's hard to believe Now I know I'm not alone In this vast unknown\n\nChorus:\nOh, and while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home And he wrote in his diary With great fanfare And he said \"This could be the start Of something unreal\"\n\nVerse 2:\nNow for ten years we've been on our own And moss grows fat on a rolling stone But that's not how it used to be When the jester sang for the king and queen In a coat he borrowed from James Dean And a voice that came from you know where Oh, and while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home\n\nChorus:\nOh, and while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home And he wrote in his diary With great fanfare And he said \"This could be the start Of something unreal\"\n\nBridge:\nAnd while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home He saw a little silhouette Of a man with long blonde hair And he said \"I'm not too sure if I know him From someplace in my past or if he's just a figment of my imagination\"\n\nChorus:\nOh, and while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home And he wrote in his diary With great fanfare And he said \"This could be the start Of something unreal\"\n\nOutro:\nAnd now for ten years we've been on our own But the kid is grown, the jester's gone And I'm older than I was When I first saw that light In the window of my cell And I know if I"}
{"k": "3983641f95fbba54e7e7c38e77f8e7e5", "t": "\n\"American Pie\" is a classic song written and recorded by Don McLean in 1971. The lyrics are poetic and open to interpretation, but here is one possible set of lyrics:\n\nVerse 1:\nA long, long time ago, I can still remember How that music used to make me smile And I knew if I had my chance That I could make those people dance And maybe they'd be happy for a while But February made me shiver With every paper I'd deliver Bad news on the doorstep And though it's hard to believe Now I know I'm not alone In this vast unknown\n\nChorus:\nOh, and while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home And he wrote in his diary With great fanfare And he said \"This could be the start Of something unreal\"\n\nVerse 2:\nNow for ten years we've been on our own And moss grows fat on a rolling stone But that's not how it used to be When the jester sang for the king and queen In a coat he borrowed from James Dean And a voice that came from you know where Oh, and while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home\n\nChorus:\nOh, and while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home And he wrote in his diary With great fanfare And he said \"This could be the start Of something unreal\"\n\nBridge:\nAnd while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home He saw a little silhouette Of a man with long blonde hair And he said \"I'm not too sure if I know him From someplace in my past or if he's just a figment of my imagination\"\n\nChorus:\nOh, and while the king was looking down On a little town called Bethlehem Two hours away from home And he wrote in his diary With great fanfare And he said \"This could be the start Of something unreal\"\n\nOutro:\nAnd now for ten years we've been on our own But the kid is grown, the jester's gone And I'm older than I was When I first saw that light In the window of my cell And I know if I"}
{"k": "a1976891dde62165507fa6ddae1c9ce2", "t": "Here are the lyrics to \"American Pie\" by Don McLean:\n\nVerse 1:\nA long, long time ago\nI can still remember\nHow that music used to make me smile\nAnd I knew if I had the chance\nWell, maybe I'd give her my answer\nYes, '52 was my first year in school\nLearning about our American history\n\nChorus:\nNow for ten years we've been on our own\nMaking good conversation\nBut just the other day, while singing \"Yankee Doodle Dandy\"\nI got a transmission that my friend had met his destiny\nAnd that made me stay home without any hesitation\nAnd while I looked around\nThere were no stars up in heaven\nAnd there was nothin' to do but ride these city streets\n\nVerse 2:\nNow, for more than two years we've been on our own\nMaking good conversation\nBut just the other day, while singing \"Yankee Doodle Dandy\"\nI got a transmission that my friend had met his destiny\nAnd that made me stay home without any hesitation\nAnd while I looked around\nThere were no stars up in heaven\nAnd there was nothin' to do but ride these city streets\n\nChorus:\nNow for ten years we've been on our own\nMaking good conversation\nBut just the other day, while singing \"Yankee Doodle Dandy\"\nI got a transmission that my friend had met his destiny\nAnd that made me stay home without any hesitation\nAnd while I looked around\nThere were no stars up in heaven\nAnd there was nothin' to do but ride these city streets\n\nVerse 3:\nNow, for more than two years we've been on our own\nMaking good conversation\nBut just the other day, while singing \"Yankee Doodle Dandy\"\nI got a transmission that my friend had met his destiny\nAnd that made me stay home without any hesitation\nAnd while I looked around\nThere were no stars up in heaven\nAnd there was nothin' to do but ride these city streets\n\nChorus:\nNow for ten years we've been on our own\nMaking good conversation\nBut just the other day, while singing \"Yankee Doodle Dandy\"\nI got a transmission that my friend had met his destiny\nAnd that made me stay home without any hesitation\nAnd while I looked around\nThere were no stars up in heaven\nAnd there was nothin' to do but ride these city streets\n\nVerse 4:\n"}
