{"k": "290b1959f1567b50a0df05ca08db967a", "t": "Sure, here are the lyrics to \"Piano Man\" by Billy Joel:\n\nVerse 1:\nIt's nine o'clock on a Saturday\nThe regular crowd shuffles in\nThere's an old man sitting next to me\nMaking love to his tonic and gin\n\nChorus:\nHe says, \"Son, can you play me a memory?\nI'm not feeling so good, I don't know what I'm doing\"\nHe says, \"Son, can you play me a memory?\"\nI say, \"Of course, man, I'm happy to try\"\n\nVerse 2:\nHe's talking with old friends he used to know\nThey're drinking and laughing and crying too\nHe's telling stories of days gone by\nAnd the memories never die\n\nChorus:\nHe says, \"Son, can you play me a memory?\nI'm not feeling so good, I don't know what I'm doing\"\nHe says, \"Son, can you play me a memory?\"\nI say, \"Of course, man, I'm happy to try\"\n\nBridge:\nI start to play and he begins to sing\nA little too loud, the music starts to sting\nBut he keeps on singing, through the good and bad\nWishing his life were like a song he's heard\n\nChorus:\nHe says, \"Son, can you play me a memory?\nI'm not feeling so good, I don't know what I'm doing\"\nHe says, \"Son, can you play me a memory?\"\nI say, \"Of course, man, I'm happy to try\"\n\nOutro:\nAnd he's singing by himself, just like before\nThe music stops and the night is still\nAnd he's left alone, with his memories once more\nTrying to find his way, through the darkness and the light."}
