{"k": "31e5d83919eda5cae57bb7f087690cd7", "t": "Sure, here's a long passage from \"Sapiens: A Brief History of Humankind\" by Yuval Noah Harari:\n\n\"The Agricultural Revolution was a turning point in human history. For the first time, humans were able to produce more food than they needed to survive. This surplus allowed them to settle in one place and engage in non-food activities such as art, science, and religion. The Agricultural Revolution marked the beginning of the rise of complex societies, where people lived together in cities and engaged in specialized labor.\n\n\"The next major turning point came with the Industrial Revolution, which began in Britain in the late 18th century. This revolution introduced new technologies such as steam engines, factories, and railways, which greatly increased productivity and allowed for mass production of goods. The Industrial Revolution transformed the economy and society, leading to the rise of industrial capitalism and the emergence of new social classes.\n\n\"The Agricultural and Industrial Revolutions had a profound impact on human history, but they also created new problems. As societies grew larger and more complex, people began to feel disconnected from their surroundings and from each other. The rise of cities led to the growth of slums and the emergence of social inequality. The Industrial Revolution also created new forms of exploitation, such as child labor and environmental degradation.\n\n\"In response to these problems, a new intellectual movement emerged in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. This movement, known as the Frankfurt School, sought to understand the social and cultural roots of these problems and to develop new forms of social critique and political action. The Frankfurt School scholars argued that capitalism was not just an economic system, but a way of life that shaped people's thoughts and feelings. They believed that the only way to challenge capitalism was to challenge its underlying ideologies and cultural norms.\n\n\"The Frankfurt School scholars were influenced by the ideas of Marx and Engels, who had argued that society was divided into two main groups: the bourgeoisie and the proletariat. The bourgeoisie owned the means of production and exploited the labor of the proletariat. The Frankfurt School scholars built on this idea by arguing that there were not just two main groups in society, but many different groups"}
