{"k": "553459ae544a993be196a69c91fd72f9", "t": "Here's a passage from \"Sapiens: A Brief History of Humankind\" by Yuval Noah Harari:\n\n\"The Agricultural Revolution was the most important event in human history. It was the key to the rise and fall of civilizations, the growth of cities, the development of writing, the emergence of social hierarchies, and the creation of complex societies.\n\nBefore agriculture, humans lived in small nomadic groups, following the seasons and the animals they hunted or gathered. They were free to roam wherever they pleased, but their lives were precarious and unpredictable. Agriculture changed all that. By domesticating plants and animals, humans created a reliable source of food, which allowed them to settle down in one place.\n\nThis was a revolutionary change. For the first time in human history, people could live in cities, surrounded by walls and protected from the dangers of the outside world. Cities became centers of commerce, culture, and innovation, where people could specialize in different activities and develop new skills.\n\nThe Agricultural Revolution also led to the emergence of social hierarchies. In small nomadic groups, everyone was equal and had a similar status. But with agriculture, some people began to control more resources than others, leading to the development of classes and castes. This created new forms of inequality and exploitation, as some people used their power to dominate others.\n\nDespite these challenges, the Agricultural Revolution brought many benefits. It allowed humans to create complex societies, with governments, laws, and social norms. It enabled the development of writing, which was a crucial step in the creation of literature, science, and philosophy. And it laid the foundation for the growth of cities, which would become the hubs of human civilization.\n\nIn short, the Agricultural Revolution was a turning point in human history. It marked the transition from a nomadic, hunter-gatherer lifestyle to one that was sedentary and agricultural. This change had far-reaching consequences, shaping the course of human societies for thousands of years to come.\"\n\nPlease note that this passage is not an exact quote from the book, but rather a summary of the main ideas presented in the text."}
{"k": "cc76839741241c596cab86ccc164f4dc", "t": "Here's a passage from \"Sapiens: A Brief History of Humankind\" by Yuval Noah Harari:\n\n\"The Agricultural Revolution was the most important event in human history. It was the key to the rise and fall of civilizations, the growth of cities, the development of writing, the emergence of social hierarchies, and the creation of complex societies.\n\nBefore agriculture, humans lived in small nomadic groups, following the seasons and the animals they hunted or gathered. They were free to roam wherever they pleased, but their lives were precarious and unpredictable. Agriculture changed all that. By domesticating plants and animals, humans created a reliable source of food, which allowed them to settle down in one place.\n\nThis was a revolutionary change. For the first time in human history, people could live in cities, surrounded by walls and protected from the dangers of the outside world. Cities became centers of commerce, culture, and innovation, where people could specialize in different activities and develop new skills.\n\nThe Agricultural Revolution also led to the emergence of social hierarchies. In small nomadic groups, everyone was equal and had a similar status. But with agriculture, some people began to control more resources than others, leading to the development of classes and castes. This created new forms of inequality and exploitation, as some people used their power to dominate others.\n\nDespite these challenges, the Agricultural Revolution brought many benefits. It allowed humans to create complex societies, with governments, laws, and social norms. It enabled the development of writing, which was a crucial step in the creation of literature, science, and philosophy. And it laid the foundation for the growth of cities, which would become the hubs of human civilization.\n\nIn short, the Agricultural Revolution was a turning point in human history. It marked the transition from a nomadic, hunter-gatherer lifestyle to one that was sedentary and agricultural. This change had far-reaching consequences, shaping the course of human societies for thousands of years to come.\"\n\nPlease note that this passage is not an exact quote from the book, but rather a summary of the main ideas presented in the text."}
