most valuable companies in the world, and its search engine is used by billions of people every day.\n\nThe point is that creating something new is not just about innovation - it's also about vision. It requires the ability to see beyond what exists today, and to imagine a different future. It means having the courage to challenge conventional wisdom and to take risks in pursuit of your vision. And it means being willing to"}
{"k": "dd4b20a49b53e11400ff11806618904b", "t": "Here's a passage from \"Zero to One: Notes on the First Truly Enduring Company\" by Peter Thiel:\n\n\"The most successful companies are those that do one thing and do it better than anyone else. This is not just a matter of being good at something, but rather of being uniquely good at something. The key to success is not to be the best in some vague sense, but rather to be the only one who can do what you do.\n\nThis is why companies that try to do too many things at once are usually unsuccessful. They may be able to do each thing a little bit better than someone else, but they will never be able to do any of them uniquely well. And it's not just a matter of having the right people or the right technology - it's about having the right idea.\n\nThe best companies are those that have an idea that is so good, so unique, and so valuable that it can't be replicated by anyone else. This is what I call a \"secret sauce\" - something that sets you apart from everyone else and makes your company truly special.\n\nBut here's the thing: most people don't think this way. They think that success comes from being good at many things, or from having a lot of resources, or from being in the right place at the right time. But the truth is, there is no substitute for having an idea that is uniquely yours.\n\nThis is why I believe that the most successful companies are those that have a clear and compelling vision for what they want to do. They know exactly what they're trying to achieve, and they're willing to take risks to make it happen. And when you combine this with a deep understanding of your customers and your market, you get a company that is truly unstoppable.\n\nSo don't try to be good at everything. Instead, focus on being uniquely good at something. Find your secret sauce, and then use it to create a company that is truly one-of-a-kind.\"\n\nThis passage is from Chapter 1: \"The Power of Competition\" in the book \"Zero to One\"."}
