ne\"."}
{"k": "3e19c18462fc59ba34c1d6de1b077001", "t": "\n Sure, here's a long passage from \"Atomic Habits\" by James Clear:\n\n\"The power of small habits lies in their ability to compound over time. A single good habit can have a noticeable impact on your life, but the real magic happens when you start building multiple habits that reinforce each other. When you create a chain of small, good habits, you unlock a powerful force that can help you achieve your goals and transform your life.\n\nThink about it like this: every habit is like a tiny little lever that you can use to move the world. Each habit is a small change that you make in your daily routine, but those small changes can have a profound impact on your life over time. For example, if you make a habit of exercising for just 10 minutes a day, that's like moving a tiny lever that will eventually turn into a giant force that propels you towards your goals.\n\nThe key to making this work is to focus on the compounding effect of small habits. Instead of trying to make big changes all at once, which can be overwhelming and difficult to maintain, focus on creating a series of small, incremental changes that you can build upon over time. For example, instead of trying to exercise for an hour every day, start by committing to just 10 minutes of exercise per day. Once you've built that habit, you can gradually increase the amount of time you spend exercising each day.\n\nThe compounding effect of small habits is like a snowball rolling down a hill. At first, it's just a tiny little ball of snow, but as it rolls down the hill, it grows and grows until it becomes a massive force that can move mountains. Similarly, each small habit you build can seem insignificant at first, but over time they will compound and create a powerful force that can help you achieve your goals.\"\n\nI hope this passage gives you a good idea of the concepts discussed in \"Atomic Habits\" by James Clear. Let me know if you have any other questions or if there's anything else I can help you with!"}
{"k": "7dd0bc997ca54167d026e602c793dd5f", "t": "\n Sure, here's a long passage from \"Atomic Habits\" by James Clear:\n\n\"The power of small habits lies in their ability to compound over time. A single good habit can have a noticeable impact on your life, but the real magic happens when you start building multiple habits that reinforce each other. When you create a chain of small, good habits, you unlock a powerful force that can help you achieve your goals and transform your life.\n\nThink about it like this: every habit is like a tiny little lever that you can use to move the world. Each habit is a small change that you make in your daily routine, but those small changes can have a profound impact on your life over time. For example, if you make a habit of exercising for just 10 minutes a day, that's like moving a tiny lever that will eventually turn into a giant force that propels you towards your goals.\n\nThe key to making this work is to focus on the compounding effect of small habits. Instead of trying to make big changes all at once, which can be overwhelming and difficult to maintain, focus on creating a series of small, incremental changes that you can build upon over time. For example, instead of trying to exercise for an hour every day, start by committing to just 10 minutes of exercise per day. Once you've built that habit, you can gradually increase the amount of time you spend exercising each day.\n\nThe compounding effect of small habits is like a snowball rolling down a hill. At first, it's just a tiny little ball of snow, but as it rolls down the hill, it grows and grows until it becomes a massive force that can move mountains. Similarly, each small habit you build can seem insignificant at first, but over time they will compound and create a powerful force that can help you achieve your goals.\"\n\nI hope this passage gives you a good idea of the concepts discussed in \"Atomic Habits\" by James Clear. Let me know if you have any other questions or if there's anything else I can help you with!"}
