{"k": "476b1c2e199475d41b50ebfb2f83353b", "t": "Here's a passage from Chapter 2: \"Space and Time\" in \"A Brief History of Time\" by Stephen Hawking:\n\n\"The idea that the universe had a beginning, and that it is still expanding, was first proposed by the Belgian priest and cosmologist Georges Lemaitre in the 1920s. This theory was later developed by the American astronomer Edwin Hubble, who discovered the expansion of the universe in the 1930s. The idea is simple: imagine a balloon being inflated. As it expands, the distance between any two points on its surface increases. Similarly, if we imagine the universe as a three-dimensional balloon, then as it expands, the distance between any two galaxies increases.\n\nThe expansion of the universe was confirmed by Hubble's observations of the light coming from distant galaxies. He found that the light was shifted towards the red end of the spectrum, a phenomenon known as redshift. This is because the light has been stretched out as it travels through space, much like the sound of a siren becomes more muffled as it recedes into the distance.\n\nThe expansion of the universe is often described as 'the galaxies moving away from each other'. But this is not entirely accurate. The galaxies are not actually moving in any particular direction; they are simply getting farther apart because space itself is expanding. Imagine two people standing on a balloon, holding hands and looking at each other. As the balloon inflates, their hands will move apart, but they themselves will remain stationary. Similarly, the galaxies are not moving through space; space is moving through them.\n\nThe expansion of the universe has an interesting consequence. If we look back in time, we see that the universe was once much denser and hotter than it is today. In fact, if we go back far enough, we reach a point known as the 'singularity', where the density and temperature are infinite. This is the moment when the universe began, and it is a point that cannot be described by our normal laws of physics.\n\nThe singularity is often referred to as the 'Big Bang'. It is a misleading name, because it implies that there was a bang, a sudden explosion, at some point in the past. But this is not what happened. The universe did not have a beginning; it simply has always existed. The Big Bang was just an event, a moment when the universe began to expand and cool down. It is a moment that we can never observe directly, because our clocks would run backwards if we went back in time to"}
