{"k": "74b581190403887bf94b1030902ca725", "t": "Here is a passage from the book \"Introduction to Algorithms\" by Thomas H. Cormen, Charles E. Leiserson, Ronald L. Rivest, and Clifford Stein:\n\n\"The problem of sorting an array of integers in ascending order is one of the most fundamental problems in computer science. It has been studied extensively for centuries, and yet it remains a crucial component of many algorithms and data structures. In this chapter, we will explore several different approaches to solving this problem.\n\nOne of the simplest and most intuitive methods for sorting an array is the insertion sort algorithm. This algorithm works by iterating through the array one element at a time, inserting each element into its proper position in a sorted subarray. The basic idea behind insertion sort is that it takes advantage of the fact that the array is already partially sorted. By inserting each new element into its correct position, we can maintain this partial ordering and eventually produce a fully sorted array.\n\nThe pseudocode for the insertion sort algorithm is as follows:\n\n```\nINSERTION-SORT(A)\n    n = length[A]\n    for i = 2 to n\n        key = A[i]\n        j = i - 1\n        while j > 0 and A[j] > key\n            A[j + 1] = A[j]\n            j = j - 1\n        A[j + 1] = key\n\n```\n\nThis algorithm has a time complexity of O(n^2), which makes it inefficient for large arrays. However, it is simple to implement and can be useful in certain situations where the array is small or partially sorted.\n\nAnother approach to sorting an array is the selection sort algorithm. This algorithm works by repeatedly finding the smallest element in the unsorted portion of the array and moving it to the beginning of the unsorted portion. The pseudocode for the selection sort algorithm is as follows:\n\n```\nSELECTION-SORT(A)\n    n = length[A]\n    for i = 1 to n - 1\n        min = i\n        for j = i + 1 to n\n            if A[j] < A[min]\n                min = j\n        swap(A[i], A[min])\n\n```\n\nThis algorithm also has a time complexity of O(n^2), which makes it inefficient for large arrays. However, like insertion sort, it is simple to implement and can be useful in certain situations where the array is small or partially sorted.\n\nIn the next chapter, we will explore more advanced sorting algorithms that have better time complexities than these two methods. We"}
