a failed idea, lean startup entrepreneurs are trained to recognize when it's time to pivot and try something new.\n\nAnother important concept in the lean startup framework is the idea of 'validated learning.' This means that entrepreneurs should always be testing their assumptions about their business with real users, rather than relying on intuition or guesswork. By validating their ideas through experimentation and customer feedback, entrepreneurs can ensure that they are building a product that truly meets the needs of their target market."}
{"k": "46c39b22b63ffdd00580c8233c149cfc", "t": "Here's a passage from \"The Lean Startup\" by Eric Ries:\n\n\"The most common mistake entrepreneurs make is to start with the wrong assumptions about their customers. They assume that they know what the customer wants, and then they build a product around those assumptions. This approach is often referred to as the 'visionary' approach, where the entrepreneur has a clear vision of what the product should be and sets out to build it.\n\nBut this approach is fundamentally flawed. It's based on the idea that you can predict what customers will want, rather than learning from them. And it's not just entrepreneurs who fall into this trap - many large companies do too. They spend millions of dollars developing a product, only to find out that nobody wants it.\n\nThe alternative is to adopt a 'customer-centric' approach, where you start by understanding the customer's needs and then build a product around those needs. This approach is often referred to as the 'lean startup' approach. It's based on the idea that you can't predict what customers will want, but you can learn from them.\n\nThe lean startup approach starts with a hypothesis about the problem you're trying to solve and the customer you're trying to serve. You then test this hypothesis by building a minimum viable product (MVP) and getting it in front of real customers as quickly as possible. The goal is not to build a perfect product, but to learn from the customers' reactions.\n\nFor example, let's say you're starting a new company that wants to create an online service for managing personal finances. You start by talking to potential customers and learning about their needs and pain points. You discover that many people are frustrated with the complexity of current financial management tools and want something simpler and more intuitive.\n\nArmed with this knowledge, you build an MVP that allows users to track their expenses and income in a simple and easy-to-use way. You then get this MVP in front of real customers as quickly as possible, either through a landing page or by offering it for free to a small group of early adopters.\n\nAs the customers start using your service, you learn what they like and don't like about it. You might discover that some users are confused by certain features, while others love the simplicity of the interface. You use this feedback to iterate on the product, making changes and improvements based on what you've learned.\n\nThis process continues until you have a product that is truly valuable to your customers. And even then, you don't stop learning. You continue to gather feedback and make adjustments as"}
