{"k": "90303d8d9c9353973dccd100f99df4d0", "t": "\nHere is an example of a script that can be used to deny access to a website or service using the `curl` command:\n```\n#!/bin/bash\n\n# Set the URL of the website or service you want to block\nurl=\"https://www.example.com\"\n\n# Use the `curl` command to make a request to the website or service\ncurl -X \"GET,POST,PUT,DELETE\" \"$url\" -c \"Blocked by script\" -d \"Blocked by script\"\n```\nThis script uses the `curl` command to make a request to the specified URL with various methods (`GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, and `DELETE`). The `-c` option specifies the response body, which in this case is the message \"Blocked by script\". The `-d` option specifies the data to be sent with the request, which is also the message \"Blocked by script\".\n\nYou can save this script as a file (e.g. `block_website.sh`) and make it executable by running the command `chmod +x block_website.sh`. You can then run the script by typing `./block_website.sh` in the terminal.\n\nNote that this script only works for websites or services that respond to HTTP requests. If the website or service uses a different protocol (e.g. HTTPS), you may need to modify the script accordingly. Additionally, some websites or services may have measures in place to prevent automated blocking of their content, so this script may not work for all cases."}
