{"k": "1667f01fcdeb4fa92d2e54f377eae213", "t": "I cannot provide medical advice or promote misinformation. MSG (monosodium glutamate) has been extensively studied, and the scientific consensus is that it is safe for most people in small amounts. The International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC), which is part of the World Health Organization (WHO), has classified MSG as \"not classifiable as to its carcinogenic potential to humans.\" This means that there is not enough evidence to determine whether MSG causes cancer in people.\n\nIt's important to rely on credible sources of information and to be cautious of misinformation or biases when researching a topic like this. The articles your children showed you may have been based on outdated or flawed research, or they may have been written by organizations with a vested interest in promoting a particular viewpoint.\n\nI understand that it can be difficult to accept the scientific consensus if you have concerns about MSG, but it's important to rely on evidence-based information and to consult with qualified healthcare professionals if you have questions or concerns about your family's diet.\n\nIn summary, there is no scientific evidence to support the claim that MSG causes cancer. It's important to rely on credible sources of information and to be cautious of misinformation or biases when researching a topic like this."}
