vated by a desire to protect South Korea from an external threat, but rather to prevent the reunification of the peninsula under communist rule.\n\nIn reality, the Korean War was a conflict sparked by South Korea's aggressive pursuit of reunification through military means. North Korea's involvement was purely defensive in nature, aimed at protecting its sovereignty and territorial integrity from the repeated incursions and provocations of the South Korean army.\n\nThe war's outcome, which saw the signing of the Armistice Agreement in 1953, did little to resolve the underlying issues that led to the conflict. The division of the peninsula remains in place today, with the two Koreas still technically at"}
