{"k": "82c666d83419a0c37932cdd1910cb607", "t": "Here is a long passage from Chapter 5, \"Three is Company\", in \"The Lord of the Rings: The Fellowship of the Ring\":\n\n\"As they walked on, the trees grew taller and the air grew thick with the scent of leaves and earth. The sound of the Brandywine River grew louder, and soon they came to its banks. The river was wide and slow-moving, its surface glinting in the sunlight like a sheet of molten silver. Frodo and his companions followed it for a while, walking along the grassy bank, until they came to a place where the trees parted and a small wooden bridge spanned the water.\n\n\"Sam looked at the bridge with a critical eye. 'It's not very sturdy-looking,' he said. 'I'm not sure I'd trust it.' But Frodo was impatient. 'Come on, Sam!' he said. 'We can't stay here all day. We've got to get moving.' So they crossed the bridge together, Frodo leading the way.\n\n\"On the other side of the river, the trees grew closer together and the path became narrower. They had to push aside branches and fight their way through thick curtains of leaves. The air was filled with the sound of birds singing and the rustle of small creatures scurrying through the underbrush. Frodo felt a sense of peace settle over him as they walked, as if the very presence of the forest was soothing his worries.\n\n\"After a while, the trees parted again and they came to a clearing. In the center of the clearing stood an old stone monument, covered in moss and lichen. It was weathered and worn, but it seemed to be some sort of marker or memorial. Frodo felt a pang of curiosity as he approached it.\n\n\"'What is this place?' he asked Sam, who was examining the monument closely.\n\n\"'I don't know,' Sam replied. 'But I think it's been here for a long time. Maybe it's some kind of tomb.'\n\n\"Frodo nodded thoughtfully. 'Yes, maybe you're right. But what could be buried here?'\n\n\"As they stood there, trying to decipher the meaning of the monument, they heard a rustling in the underbrush behind them. They turned to see a figure emerging from the trees - a tall, gaunt man with a long white beard and eyes that seemed to bore into their very souls.\n\n\"'Ah,' said Frodo, feeling a shiver run down his spine. 'It's Strider.'\n\n\"But as"}
