{"k": "2eb82f21b545e5a01e624e32186a7a73", "t": "Here is a passage from Chapter 10 of \"To Kill a Mockingbird\" by Harper Lee:\n\n\"As I lay in bed, I could hear the mockingbirds outside my window. They were singing their little hearts out, and I was listening to them with great pleasure. It was a warm summer evening, and the air was filled with the sweet scent of blooming flowers. I had just finished reading a book about the Civil War, and it had left me feeling sad and thoughtful.\n\nAs I lay there, I couldn't help but think about Tom Robinson, the black man who had been falsely accused of raping Mayella Ewell. Mr. Finch was defending him in court, and I knew that he would do his best to prove Tom's innocence. But I also knew that it wouldn't be easy. The whole town seemed to be against Tom, and I worried about what might happen if Mr. Finch lost the case.\n\nJust then, I heard a faint humming noise coming from outside my window. It was Scout, my little sister, singing along with the mockingbirds. She had always loved music, and she would often sing along with the birds when they were in season. I smiled to myself as I listened to her sweet voice blending with the mockingbirds'.\n\nAs I lay there, feeling grateful for the simple joys in life, I heard a knock at my door. It was Jem, my older brother. He looked tired and worried, and I could tell that something was bothering him.\n\n\"What's wrong, Jem?\" I asked, sitting up in bed.\n\n\"It's just...I don't know if Mr. Finch can win this case,\" he said, looking at me with a serious expression. \"The whole town is against Tom, and I'm afraid of what might happen to him if we lose.\"\n\nI nodded sympathetically. I knew that Jem was worried about the same things I was.\n\n\"Don't worry, Jem,\" I said. \"Mr. Finch will do his best to prove Tom's innocence. And even if we don't win, we'll still have each other and our family to rely on.\"\n\nJem nodded, looking a little better. We sat there in silence for a moment, listening to the mockingbirds outside.\n\nThen Jem spoke up again. \"You know, Scout, I've been thinking about something,\" he said. \"I think it's time we started learning more about Boo Radley. He's always been a mystery to us, and I think it would be interesting to learn"}
