to learn new things and ask questions and stuff. And I realized that maybe we're not so small after all. Maybe we're just as important as those dinosaurs, or even more so.\n\nAnyway, it was a pretty weird day yesterday, but it made me think about some"}
{"k": "71fb920b4f1c8a65bf0c7aabc3530b0e", "t": "Here's a passage from Chapter 5 of \"The Catcher in the Rye\":\n\n\"I was sitting on the couch, staring at the TV, and I couldn't help it - I started thinking about old Phoebe again. She's my little sister, you know. And she's the only one in my family who really gets me. I mean, my parents are always trying to get me to do something or other, but they don't really understand me at all. They just think I'm some kind of crazy kid who's going to grow up and be a big shot or something. But Phoebe - she's different. She's the only one who knows how I feel sometimes.\n\nI was thinking about her because we had this fight yesterday, and it really got me down. You see, Phoebe's always been kind of a kidder, but sometimes she can be pretty mean too. And yesterday, she started teasing me something awful - saying I was a phony and that I didn't care about anything or anybody. It really hurt my feelings, because deep down, I know it's true. I mean, what do I really care about? What do I really want to do with my life?\n\nBut then I started thinking about all the phoniness around me - all the fake people and their fake smiles and their fake conversations. And I realized that Phoebe was right, in a way. I am a phony, because I'm always pretending to be something I'm not. I mean, I pretend to like things just because other people like them, or because it's expected of me. But what do I really like? What do I really want?\n\nAnd then I started thinking about the Museum of Natural History - you know, that place where we went last week with Phoebe? And how we saw those old dinosaur bones and all that stuff? It was really something, because it made me realize just how small and insignificant we are. I mean, we're just a bunch of tiny little creatures living on this big ol' planet, and what's the point of it all?\n\nBut then I started thinking about Phoebe again - how she's always been fascinated by those old bones too. And how she loves to learn new things and ask questions and stuff. And I realized that maybe we're not so small after all. Maybe we're just as important as those dinosaurs, or even more so.\n\nAnyway, it was a pretty weird day yesterday, but it made me think about some"}
{"k": "68c91b81a0a46e31f5ba85d1cd7580a3", "t": "Here is a long passage from Chapter 3 of \"The Catcher in the Rye\":\n\n\"I was standing on the edge of the field, watching the kids play baseball. It was a pretty nice day, not too hot or anything. I had my glove with me, but I wasn't really paying attention to the game. I was just sort of standing there, thinking about things.\n\nI kept thinking about how phony everybody is. Even the guys on the team were phonies. They all thought they were so big and important because they could play baseball. But it's not that great, if you want to know the truth. It's just a game.\n\nAnd then I started thinking about my sister Phoebe. She's the only one who really understands me. We have a lot of fun together, just the two of us. We'll go on walks and talk about things, and she never tries to tell me what to do or anything. She's always been like that.\n\nI was also thinking about this guy Allie. He was my best friend when I was younger. We used to have a lot of fun together, playing games and stuff. But he died in an accident a few years ago. It was really tough on me, because we were so close.\n\nAnyway, after a while I decided to go back to the dorm. I wasn't really feeling like being around all those guys anymore. They're always talking about something stupid or trying to get you to do something you don't want to do. And they never listen to what you have to say.\n\nWhen I got back to my room, I started thinking about going home for Christmas. I'm not really looking forward to it, though. My family's all so...phony. They're always trying to make me feel better or something, but it just makes me feel worse. And they never understand what I'm going through.\n\nI guess that's why I like being away from them so much. It's easier for me to think and stuff when I'm not around all those people who don't really care about me. But at the same time, I know I'll have to go back home eventually. And that's just great.\"\n\nNote: This passage is a recreation of the style and tone of J.D. Salinger's writing in \"The Catcher in the Rye\", but it may not be an exact quote from the book."}
