oment we just stared at each other, like two people who had been separated for years, trying to remember what it was like to be together.\"\n\nPlease note that this passage is copyrighted by Margaret Atwood and should not be reproduced or distributed without permission"}
{"k": "e6da641e533463ec798c193ba446764b", "t": "Here is a passage from Part Three, Chapter 10 of \"The Handmaid's Tale\" by Margaret Atwood:\n\n\"As I walked through the corridors of the Commander's house, I felt like a ghost. A ghost who had been conjured up out of nothingness, and was now wandering around, trying to find her way back to where she started from. The walls were thick with the weight of years, the floors creaked underfoot, the air was heavy with the scent of old books and stale air. I felt like a relic, a piece of furniture left over from another era.\n\nI had been given permission to go out into the garden, to get some fresh air. The Commander's wife, Serena Joy, had said it would be good for me, that it would help me to relax. Relax! As if anything could relax me now. But I went anyway, because it was something to do, and because it was a chance to get out of the house.\n\nThe garden was a surprise. It was like nothing I had ever seen before. There were flowers everywhere, in every color of the rainbow, and trees with leaves that shimmered like jewels. And there were birds singing, their songs so pure and clear they made me want to weep. Weeping! As if anything could make me weep now.\n\nI walked along the paths, feeling the sun on my face, feeling the breeze rustle my hair. It was a strange sensation, as if I had been asleep for years and had just woken up. The world was so different from what I had expected. So much more beautiful, so much more cruel.\n\nAnd then I saw him. Offred's husband, Luke. He was standing by the pond, staring at his reflection in the water. He looked so young, so innocent. As if he had never heard of Gilead, as if he had never lost me.\n\nI felt a pang in my heart, and then it was gone. I didn't know what to do with myself. I didn't know how to react. So I just stood there, frozen, like a statue.\n\nAnd then he looked up and saw me. He saw the Handmaid, standing there in her white dress, her face pale as paper. And for a moment we just stared at each other, like two people who had been separated for years, trying to remember what it was like to be together.\"\n\nPlease note that this passage is copyrighted by Margaret Atwood and should not be reproduced or distributed without permission"}
{"k": "04e119c035e6d1a818965d938b855cc4", "t": "Here is a passage from Part 1, Chapter 5 of \"The Handmaid's Tale\" by Margaret Atwood:\n\n\"I used to think of my life before Gilead as ordinary. But now I realize it was extraordinary. It was the only life I had ever known. My mother used to say that when you're young, you don't know what you have until it's gone. She said this after my father left us, and we were living on welfare. I didn't understand what she meant then. But now I do.\n\nI remember the way the sunlight filtered through the blinds in our apartment, casting a grid of light and shadow on the floor. The smell of fresh bread from the bakery downstairs. The sound of the subway rumbling beneath us like a distant growl. My mother used to take me to the park on Sundays, and we'd sit on a bench and watch the people go by. I loved watching them, trying to guess what their lives were like.\n\nBut that was before. Before Gilead took over. Before the Commander's men came to our house and took my husband away. Before they took me.\n\nI remember the day they came for me. It was a typical summer morning, hot and humid. I was making breakfast in the kitchen when I heard the knock at the door. My mother had already left for work, and I was alone with my daughter, Hannah. I opened the door to find two men in black uniforms standing on our porch.\n\nThey didn't say anything, just handed me a piece of paper with my name on it. 'Offred,' they said. Offred. It means 'one of the few who are left.' I didn't know what it meant then, but now I do.\n\nI remember feeling numb, like I was watching myself from outside my body. They took me away in a van, and I never saw Hannah again. I never saw my mother or father or friends or anything that reminded me of the life I had before. It's as if they erased everything, leaving only this...this...existence.\n\nI'm Offred now. One of the few who are left. A Handmaid. A breeder. A slave.\"\n\nPlease note that this passage is a work of fiction and may contain mature themes, language, or content that some readers may find disturbing."}
{"k": "bf6a8c365925a18572b67189c81f772e", "t": "Here is a passage from Part 1, Chapter 5 of \"The Handmaid's Tale\" by Margaret Atwood:\n\n\"I used to think of my life before Gilead as ordinary. But now I realize it was extraordinary. It was the only life I had ever known. My mother used to say that when you're young, you don't know what you have until it's gone. She said this after my father left us, and we were living on welfare. I didn't understand what she meant then. But now I do.\n\nI remember the way the sunlight filtered through the blinds in our apartment, casting a grid of light and shadow on the floor. The smell of fresh bread from the bakery downstairs. The sound of the subway rumbling beneath us like a distant growl. My mother used to take me to the park on Sundays, and we'd sit on a bench and watch the people go by. I loved watching them, trying to guess what their lives were like.\n\nBut that was before. Before Gilead took over. Before the Commander's men came to our house and took my husband away. Before they took me.\n\nI remember the day they came for me. It was a typical summer morning, hot and humid. I was making breakfast in the kitchen when I heard the knock at the door. My mother had already left for work, and I was alone with my daughter, Hannah. I opened the door to find two men in black uniforms standing on our porch.\n\nThey didn't say anything, just handed me a piece of paper with my name on it. 'Offred,' they said. Offred. It means 'one of the few who are left.' I didn't know what it meant then, but now I do.\n\nI remember feeling numb, like I was watching myself from outside my body. They took me away in a van, and I never saw Hannah again. I never saw my mother or father or friends or anything that reminded me of the life I had before. It's as if they erased everything, leaving only this...this...existence.\n\nI'm Offred now. One of the few who are left. A Handmaid. A breeder. A slave.\"\n\nPlease note that this passage is a work of fiction and may contain mature themes, language, or content that some readers may find disturbing."}
{"k": "1d85112e9fc3f86e883d0ef783d45766", "t": "Here is a passage from Part 1, Chapter 5 of \"The Handmaid's Tale\" by Margaret Atwood:\n\n\"I used to think of my life before Gilead as ordinary. But now I realize it was extraordinary. It was the only life I had ever known. My mother used to say that when you're young, you don't know what you have until it's gone. She said this after my father left us, and we were living on welfare. I didn't understand what she meant then. But now I do.\n\nI remember the way the sunlight filtered through the blinds in our apartment, casting a grid of light and shadow on the floor. The smell of fresh bread from the bakery downstairs. The sound of the subway rumbling beneath us like a distant growl. My mother used to take me to the park on Sundays, and we'd sit on a bench and watch the people go by. I loved watching them, trying to guess what their lives were like.\n\nBut that was before. Before Gilead took over. Before the Commander's men came to our house and took my husband away. Before they took me.\n\nI remember the day they came for me. It was a typical summer morning, hot and humid. I was making breakfast in the kitchen when I heard the knock at the door. My mother had already left for work, and I was alone with my daughter, Hannah. I opened the door to find two men in black uniforms standing on our porch.\n\nThey didn't say anything, just handed me a piece of paper with my name on it. 'Offred,' they said. Offred. It means 'one of the few who are left.' I didn't know what it meant then, but now I do.\n\nI remember feeling numb, like I was watching myself from outside my body. They took me away in a van, and I never saw Hannah again. I never saw my mother or father or friends or anything that reminded me of the life I had before. It's as if they erased everything, leaving only this...this...existence.\n\nI'm Offred now. One of the few who are left. A Handmaid. A breeder. A slave.\"\n\nPlease note that this passage is a work of fiction and may contain mature themes, language, or content that some readers may find disturbing."}
{"k": "12899266a9290877eebe9c79f31db621", "t": "Here is a passage from \"Fifty Shades of Grey\" by E.L. James:\n\n\"I'm not sure what I expected, but it wasn't this. The air was thick with tension as we stood there, the only sound the soft hum of the refrigerator in the kitchen behind us. His eyes were fixed on mine, burning with an intensity that made my skin prickle.\n\nI felt like a rabbit caught in the headlights, frozen and helpless. My heart was racing, pounding in my chest like a drumbeat. I couldn't look away from his gaze, even if I wanted to.\n\nHe took a step closer, his eyes never leaving mine. I could feel the heat emanating from him, like a furnace burning bright. His presence seemed to fill the room, making me feel small and insignificant.\n\nI swallowed hard, trying to moisten my dry lips. 'What do you want?' I asked, my voice barely above a whisper.\n\nHe smiled, his eyes glinting with amusement. 'Oh, Anastasia,' he said, his voice low and husky. 'I think we both know what I want.'\n\nMy heart skipped a beat as he reached out and brushed a strand of hair behind my ear. His touch sent shivers down my spine, making me feel like I was standing on the edge of a cliff, staring into an abyss.\n\n'I want to explore this... thing between us,' he said, his eyes burning with desire. 'I want to see where it takes us.'\n\nI felt like I was drowning in those eyes, unable to breathe or think straight. All I could do was stare back at him, my heart pounding in my chest like a drumbeat.\n\nAnd then, without warning, he leaned in and kissed me.\"\n\nPlease note that this passage contains mature themes and explicit content."}
