{"k": "df6e5461e6b79750c637adc15e6311b2", "t": "Here is a long passage from Chapter 5 of \"The Lion, the Witch, and the Wardrobe\":\n\n\"As they walked on, the trees grew taller and the air grew colder. They could see that they were approaching the edge of the forest, for the trees began to thin out and the underbrush grew less dense. The children's breath misted in the chill air as they quickened their pace.\n\n'Look!' said Lucy suddenly, stopping short and pointing ahead. 'What is it?' asked Edmund, but Lucy didn't answer. She was staring at something on the ground.\n\n'There's a path,' she said finally. 'I think we'd better follow it.'\n\nThe path wound its way through the trees, leading them deeper into the forest. The children walked in silence for a few minutes, their eyes fixed on the ground ahead. Then Edmund spoke up.\n\n'I don't like this place,' he said. 'It's so...so...frightening.'\n\n'What do you mean?' asked Lucy.\n\n'I don't know,' said Edmund. 'I just feel it. It feels...it feels like we're being watched.'\n\nLucy looked around, but she couldn't see anything out of the ordinary. The trees seemed to loom over them, casting long shadows on the ground, but that was all.\n\n'Let's hurry up and get out of here,' said Edmund.\n\nBut Lucy didn't move. She stood stock still, her eyes fixed on something ahead.\n\n'What is it?' asked Peter, who had been walking behind her.\n\nLucy pointed. 'Look!' she said.\n\nAs they followed her gaze, they saw that the path opened out into a clearing. In the center of the clearing stood an enormous stone table, its surface worn smooth by time and weather. And on top of the table lay a great white stag, its antlers reaching up towards the sky like a crown.\n\nThe children stared in wonder at the sight before them. They had never seen anything so beautiful, so majestic, in their lives. But as they gazed, they began to feel a strange sense of unease. It was as if something was watching them from behind the stag's antlers, something that didn't want them there.\n\n'Let's go back,' whispered Edmund, tugging at Lucy's arm.\n\nBut Lucy stood frozen, her eyes fixed on the stag. She seemed to be under some sort of spell, unable to move or speak.\"\n\nI hope this passage meets your request! Let me know if you'd"}
