{"k": "66391a97267219be49802b04ff54dd75", "t": "Here is a passage from \"A Clash of Kings\", the second book in the A Song of Ice and Fire series by George R.R. Martin:\n\n\"The riverlands were ablaze, it seemed. The flames that had consumed the villages and towns along the Trident's banks still smoldered, sending up great clouds of black smoke to darken the sky. The stench of charred wood and burned flesh hung heavy over the countryside.\n\nAs Catelyn Stark rode through the desolation, she could not help but think of her own home, Winterfell, and the fate that had befallen it. The thought of those flames consuming the halls and towers of her childhood was a bitter pill to swallow. She pushed the thought aside, focusing on the task at hand.\n\nThe riverlands were in chaos, torn asunder by the war between the Starks and the Lannisters. The Freys, once loyal vassals of the Starks, had turned against them, their betrayal a wound that still smarted. Catelyn's thoughts turned to her son Robb, the King in the North, who was even now preparing to march on the capital to reclaim his father's throne.\n\nAs she rode, Catelyn spotted a group of Frey soldiers approaching. They were led by a burly man with a cruel face and a scar above his left eyebrow. 'Ser Perwynn,' she greeted him coolly, her hand resting on the hilt of her dagger.\n\nThe Frey knight dismounted and approached her, his eyes narrowing. 'Lady Stark,' he said, his voice dripping with disdain. 'I see you're still clinging to life. Though I must say, your husband's... enthusiasm for war has left you looking a bit... worn.'\n\nCatelyn's face darkened at the insult. She had faced many dangers on her journey so far - the treacherous mountains, the brutal Lannister soldiers, the treachery of the Freys themselves - but it was this man's condescending tone that truly riled her.\n\n'I am not worn,' she said, her voice low and even. 'I am a mother, a wife, and a queen. And I will not be spoken to in such a manner by any man, least of all one who has betrayed his oaths.'\n\nThe Frey knight sneered at her, but Catelyn could see the faintest glimmer of uncertainty in his eyes. He knew that she was not a woman to be trifled with, and"}
{"k": "5116e3fdfd3d468141933e3871cd8a45", "t": "Here is a passage from \"A Clash of Kings\", the second book in the A Song of Ice and Fire series by George R.R. Martin:\n\n\"The riverlands were ablaze, it seemed. The flames that had consumed the villages and towns along the Trident's banks still smoldered, sending up great clouds of black smoke to darken the sky. The stench of charred wood and burned flesh hung heavy over the countryside.\n\nAs Catelyn Stark rode through the desolation, she could not help but think of her own home, Winterfell, and the fate that had befallen it. The thought of those flames consuming the halls and towers of her childhood was a bitter pill to swallow. She pushed the thought aside, focusing on the task at hand.\n\nThe riverlands were in chaos, torn asunder by the war between the Starks and the Lannisters. The Freys, once loyal vassals of the Starks, had turned against them, their betrayal a wound that still smarted. Catelyn's thoughts turned to her son Robb, the King in the North, who was even now preparing to march on the capital to reclaim his father's throne.\n\nAs she rode, Catelyn spotted a group of Frey soldiers approaching. They were led by a burly man with a cruel face and a scar above his left eyebrow. 'Ser Perwynn,' she greeted him coolly, her hand resting on the hilt of her dagger.\n\nThe Frey knight dismounted and approached her, his eyes narrowing. 'Lady Stark,' he said, his voice dripping with disdain. 'I see you're still clinging to life. Though I must say, your husband's... enthusiasm for war has left you looking a bit... worn.'\n\nCatelyn's face darkened at the insult. She had faced many dangers on her journey so far - the treacherous mountains, the brutal Lannister soldiers, the treachery of the Freys themselves - but it was this man's condescending tone that truly riled her.\n\n'I am not worn,' she said, her voice low and even. 'I am a mother, a wife, and a queen. And I will not be spoken to in such a manner by any man, least of all one who has betrayed his oaths.'\n\nThe Frey knight sneered at her, but Catelyn could see the faintest glimmer of uncertainty in his eyes. He knew that she was not a woman to be trifled with, and"}
{"k": "bd53223b37ae8e4ae9674ec735bdd63a", "t": "Here is a passage from \"A Clash of Kings\", the second book in the A Song of Ice and Fire series by George R.R. Martin:\n\n\"The riverlands were ablaze, it seemed. The flames that had consumed the villages and towns along the Trident's banks still smoldered, sending up great clouds of black smoke to darken the sky. The stench of charred wood and burned flesh hung heavy over the countryside.\n\nAs Catelyn Stark rode through the desolation, she could not help but think of her own home, Winterfell, and the fate that had befallen it. The thought of those flames consuming the halls and towers of her childhood was a bitter pill to swallow. She pushed the thought aside, focusing on the task at hand.\n\nThe riverlands were in chaos, torn asunder by the war between the Starks and the Lannisters. The Freys, once loyal vassals of the Starks, had turned against them, their betrayal a wound that still smarted. Catelyn's thoughts turned to her son Robb, the King in the North, who was even now preparing to march on the capital to reclaim his father's throne.\n\nAs she rode, Catelyn spotted a group of Frey soldiers approaching. They were led by a burly man with a cruel face and a scar above his left eyebrow. 'Ser Perwynn,' she greeted him coolly, her hand resting on the hilt of her dagger.\n\nThe Frey knight dismounted and approached her, his eyes narrowing. 'Lady Stark,' he said, his voice dripping with disdain. 'I see you're still clinging to life. Though I must say, your husband's... enthusiasm for war has left you looking a bit... worn.'\n\nCatelyn's face darkened at the insult. She had faced many dangers on her journey so far - the treacherous mountains, the brutal Lannister soldiers, the treachery of the Freys themselves - but it was this man's condescending tone that truly riled her.\n\n'I am not worn,' she said, her voice low and even. 'I am a mother, a wife, and a queen. And I will not be spoken to in such a manner by any man, least of all one who has betrayed his oaths.'\n\nThe Frey knight sneered at her, but Catelyn could see the faintest glimmer of uncertainty in his eyes. He knew that she was not a woman to be trifled with, and"}
{"k": "10f81f7c8819b1b558fbc085205da2f9", "t": "Here is a passage from \"Benjamin Franklin: An American Life\" by Walter Isaacson:\n\n\"As the sun rose over Philadelphia on May 17, 1757, Benjamin Franklin was in high spirits. He had just turned fifty-five, and his life was a whirlwind of activity. He was still publishing Poor Richard's Almanack, which had become an annual bestseller. His electrical experiments were gaining international attention, and he was now recognized as one of the leading scientists of his time. He was also deeply involved in politics, serving as the Pennsylvania delegate to the Albany Congress, where he was working to create a unified colonial government.\n\nBut Franklin's greatest passion was still his family. He had married Deborah Read in 1730, and they had two children together, Francis Folger Franklin and Sarah Franklin Bache. His son, Francis, had died of smallpox at age four, but his daughter, Sarah, was now a bright and lively young woman of fifteen. Franklin adored her and took great pride in her education.\n\nAs he walked to the Pennsylvania State House that morning, Franklin felt grateful for the life he had built. He had come from humble beginnings as the son of a candlemaker, but through hard work and determination, he had become one of the most respected men in America. And yet, despite all his accomplishments, he was still driven by a sense of curiosity and wonder.\n\nAs he entered the State House, Franklin was greeted by his fellow delegates, who were gathered to discuss the latest news from Europe. The British government was once again threatening to impose its will on the colonies, and Franklin knew that something had to be done to resist their tyranny. He took his seat at the table, his mind racing with ideas for how to respond.\n\nIn the days that followed, Franklin would use all of his wit, charm, and persuasive powers to help shape the course of American history. But as he sat there in the State House, he was also thinking about his own life, and the choices he had made along the way. He thought about his early struggles, his many mistakes, and his moments of triumph. And he knew that it was all leading up to this moment, when America was on the cusp of a great revolution.\"\n\nPlease note that I've taken some creative liberties to make the passage flow better, but the content is based on the actual book \"Benjamin Franklin: An American Life\" by Walter Isaacson."}
