just important for individuals; it's also crucial for businesses and governments. When a company decides to invest in a new project, it gives up the opportunity to use those resources elsewhere. The opportunity cost of that investment is the value of the best alternative project that could have been undertaken with those same resources.\n\nSimilarly, when a government decides to spend money on one program rather than another, it gives up the opportunity to use those resources for something else. The opportunity cost of that decision is the value of"}
{"k": "87d69e614ba1a8f52559f8a8cfeff895", "t": "Here is a passage from Chapter 3: The Market for Labor in the book \"Principles of Economics\" by N. Gregory Mankiw:\n\n**The Supply Curve**\n\nIn this chapter, we have seen how to derive the demand curve for labor. But what about the supply curve? To understand the supply curve, let's consider the decision that a worker makes when deciding whether or not to work.\n\nImagine that you are a college student who is considering working part-time while attending school. You have skills and abilities that make you productive in the labor market, but you also have other uses for your time, such as studying for exams and participating in extracurricular activities. Suppose that you value each hour of leisure (i.e., not working) at $10 per hour.\n\nNow suppose that a local restaurant offers to pay you $12 per hour to work as a waiter. In this case, the opportunity cost of your time is less than its value to you. If you choose to work for the restaurant, you will give up some leisure time, but you will earn more than the value you place on that leisure time.\n\nThe supply curve shows how much labor people are willing and able to supply at each wage rate. In this example, your decision to work as a waiter is represented by a point on the supply curve. The horizontal axis measures the quantity of labor supplied (in hours), and the vertical axis measures the wage rate (in dollars per hour). If you choose to work 20 hours per week for the restaurant, then your supply curve shows that at a wage rate of $12 per hour, you are willing to supply 20 hours of labor.\n\nBut what happens if the wage rate rises? Suppose the restaurant offers to pay you $15 per hour. In this case, the opportunity cost of your time is even lower than before. You will still value each hour of leisure at $10 per hour, but now you can earn more than that by working for the restaurant. As a result, you are willing and able to supply more labor: 25 hours per week.\n\nNotice what has happened here. The wage rate has increased from $12 to $15 per hour, and as a result, the quantity of labor supplied has increased from 20 to 25 hours per week. This is an example of how the supply curve slopes upward: when the wage rate rises, the quantity of labor supplied also increases.\n\nThe supply curve shows that people are willing and able to supply more labor at higher wage rates because they value their leisure"}
