{"k": "41cc284157481ca9db14cf2a60b07a5f", "t": "Here is a passage from \"Little Fires Everywhere\" by Celeste Ng:\n\n\"The fire department arrived at 2:47 AM, sirens blaring and lights flashing like a beacon in the darkness. The first responders jumped out of their trucks and ran towards the burning house, shouting to each other as they went. Mia Warren watched them from her own front porch, her heart racing with every step.\n\nShe had been woken by the sound of shattering glass, followed by the unmistakable crackle of flames. At first, she thought it was just the usual summer storms that seemed to plague their small town, but then she saw the smoke billowing into the air and knew something was wrong.\n\nAs she watched, a figure emerged from the burning house - a young girl, her face smudged with soot and her eyes wide with fear. Mia's heart skipped a beat as she recognized the girl: it was Izzy Richardson, the daughter of the Richardsons' oldest son, Moody. Mia had babysat for Izzy countless times, and she knew that Moody and his wife, Linda, were out of town on vacation.\n\nThe fire department quickly got to work, spraying foam and water onto the burning house as they tried to put out the flames. Mia watched in horror as the roof began to collapse, sending sparks flying into the air like a thousand tiny stars. She felt a lump form in her throat as she thought about all the memories that were being destroyed - the laughter, the tears, the countless nights spent around the dinner table.\n\nAs the firefighters worked to contain the blaze, Mia's mind began to spin with questions. Who had started the fire? And why? Was it an accident, or was someone trying to send a message? She knew that the Richardsons were a tight-knit family, but she also knew that they weren't immune to the same problems and struggles that plagued everyone else in their small town.\n\nAs the sun began to rise, casting a golden glow over the devastation, Mia felt a sense of unease settle into her bones. This was more than just a house fire - it was a wake-up call, a reminder that even the most seemingly perfect families could be hiding secrets and struggles behind closed doors.\"\n\nPlease note that this passage is not an exact quote from the book, but rather my attempt to generate a passage in the style of Celeste Ng's writing."}
