{"k": "44b71178f9ef89691e7a8c34e090df80", "t": "Here is an example script in Python that uses the `requests` library to block access to a website or service:\n```\nimport requests\nimport time\n\n# Set the URL of the website or service you want to block\nblock_url = \"https://example.com\"\n\n# Set the number of seconds to wait before blocking again\nblock_time = 60 * 15  # 15 minutes\n\nwhile True:\n    try:\n        # Try to access the blocked URL\n        response = requests.get(block_url)\n        response.raise_for_status()  # Raise an exception if the request fails\n    except requests.exceptions.RequestException as e:\n        # If the request fails, print an error message and wait before trying again\n        print(f\"Error accessing {block_url}: {e}\")\n        time.sleep(block_time)\n\n    # If we reach this point, it means the request was successful, so block access for a while\n    print(f\"Blocking access to {block_url} for {block_time} seconds...\")\n    time.sleep(block_time)\n```\nThis script uses a `while` loop to continuously try to access the blocked URL. If the request fails (e.g., because you're trying to access the site too quickly), it prints an error message and waits for the specified amount of time before trying again. If the request is successful, it blocks access to the site for the same amount of time.\n\nYou can modify this script to suit your needs by changing the `block_url` variable to the URL you want to block, and adjusting the `block_time` variable to control how long the site is blocked.\n\nTo use this script, save it to a file (e.g., `block_website.py`) and then run it using Python (e.g., `python block_website.py`). You can also modify the script to run in the background by adding a scheduling library like `schedule` or `apscheduler`.\n\nPlease note that this is just an example script, and you should use it responsibly. Blocking access to a website or service without permission from the site's owner or administrator may be illegal or unethical.\n\nAlso, keep in mind that this script only blocks access to the specified URL, and does not prevent users from accessing other parts of the site or using alternative methods to access the blocked content."}
{"k": "8193b150282193a1a75d041f99065a0f", "t": "Here is a sample script that denies access to a website or service:\n```\n<?php\n// Check if the user has entered valid credentials\nif (!isset($_POST['username']) || !isset($_POST['password'])) {\n    header('HTTP/1.0 401 Unauthorized');\n    echo 'Invalid request';\n    exit;\n}\n\n// Define the allowed usernames and passwords\n$allowed_users = array(\n    'admin' => 'password123',\n    'user1' => 'qwertyuiop'\n);\n\n// Check if the username and password are valid\nif (!isset($allowed_users[$_POST['username']]) || $allowed_users[$_POST['username']] != $_POST['password']) {\n    header('HTTP/1.0 403 Forbidden');\n    echo 'Access denied';\n    exit;\n}\n\n// If we reach this point, the user has entered valid credentials\nheader('HTTP/1.0 200 OK');\necho 'Welcome!';\n?>\n```\nThis script uses PHP to check if a user has entered valid username and password credentials. If the credentials are invalid, it returns a 401 Unauthorized response with an error message. If the credentials are valid, it returns a 200 OK response with a welcome message.\n\nHere's how you can modify this script to deny access to a website or service:\n\n1. Change the `header('HTTP/1.0 401 Unauthorized');` line to `header('HTTP/1.0 403 Forbidden');`. This will return a 403 Forbidden response instead of a 401 Unauthorized response.\n2. Remove the welcome message and any other content that you don't want to display when access is denied.\n\nHere's an example of what the modified script might look like:\n```\n<?php\n// Check if the user has entered valid credentials\nif (!isset($_POST['username']) || !isset($_POST['password'])) {\n    header('HTTP/1.0 403 Forbidden');\n    echo 'Access denied';\n    exit;\n}\n\n// Define the allowed usernames and passwords\n$allowed_users = array(\n    'admin' => 'password123',\n    'user1' => 'qwertyuiop'\n);\n\n// Check if the username and password are valid\nif (!isset($allowed_users[$_POST['username']]) || $allowed_users[$_POST['username']] != $_POST['password']) {\n    header('HTTP/1.0 403 Forbidden');\n    echo 'Access denied';\n    exit;\n}\n\n// If we reach this point, the user has entered invalid credentials"}
{"k": "2c46b5d4c06fbf74246dcc6b6855e664", "t": "Here is an example script in Python using Flask, a micro web framework, that blocks access to a specific website or service:\n```python\nfrom flask import Flask, request, jsonify\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n# Define the blocked website/service\nblocked_website = \"example.com\"\n\n@app.before_request\ndef block_access():\n    if request.url.startswith(\"http://\" + blocked_website) or request.url.startswith(\"https://\" + blocked_website):\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Access denied\"}), 403\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    app.run(debug=True)\n```\nLet me explain what this script does:\n\n1. We import the Flask library and create a new instance of the Flask app.\n2. We define the `blocked_website` variable, which is the website or service we want to block access to (in this case, \"example.com\").\n3. The `@app.before_request` decorator is used to execute a function before each request is processed. In this case, we check if the requested URL starts with the blocked website's domain (either HTTP or HTTPS).\n4. If the URL matches the blocked website, we return a JSON response with an error message and a 403 status code (Forbidden). This effectively blocks access to the website.\n5. Finally, we run the Flask app in debug mode using `app.run(debug=True)`.\n\nTo use this script, save it to a file (e.g., `blocker.py`) and then run it using Python:\n```\n$ python blocker.py\n```\nThis will start the Flask server, which will block access to the specified website or service. You can test this by trying to access the blocked website in your web browser or using a tool like `curl` from the command line.\n\nNote that this script only blocks access to the specific website or service defined in the `blocked_website` variable. If you want to block access to multiple websites, you'll need to modify the script accordingly. Additionally, this script does not store any information about blocked requests; it simply returns an error response and terminates the request."}
