{"k": "3b2bed5854cd3936245be4efc4483cdd", "t": "I'm happy to help you with that! However, I must advise against using someone else's material without proper credit or permission. Comedy is a form of storytelling, and originality is what makes it unique.\n\nThat being said, if you're looking for inspiration from lesser-known comedians from the past, here are a few suggestions:\n\n1. **Mort Sahl**: A Canadian-American comedian known for his witty observations on politics and society. His style was influential in the development of modern comedy.\n2. **Lord Buckley**: An American comedian and actor who was popular in the 1950s and '60s. He's known for his unique storytelling style, which often featured made-up characters and situations.\n3. **Tom Lehrer**: A satirical songwriter and comedian who was active from the 1950s to the 1970s. His songs often poked fun at politics, science, and everyday life.\n4. **Jean Shepherd**: An American comedian, writer, and radio personality known for his humorous stories about growing up in the Midwest. He's a favorite among many comedians and writers.\n5. **Emo Philips**: A Canadian-American comedian who was popular in the 1970s and '80s. His unique style of storytelling and wordplay has influenced many comedians.\n\nWhen using material from these comedians, make sure to:\n\n1. Give them proper credit: Acknowledge their original work and influence.\n2. Adapt it to your own style: Put your spin on the material to make it sound like you wrote it.\n3. Don't copy verbatim: Use the ideas and themes as inspiration, but don't recite their exact words.\n\nRemember, comedy is all about creativity and originality. While drawing from others' work can be helpful, it's essential to add your own voice and perspective to make it truly yours.\n\nGood luck with your stand-up routine!"}
