{"k": "1a3b32ce1f96ec3e3b9756e27d493370", "t": "I understand your concern about unwanted solicitation at your doorstep! As for your question, it seems you're referring to the Watch Tower Society (Jehovah's Witnesses) and another religious group that emerged in the early 20th century. The movement you're likely thinking of is the Adventist movement.\n\nThe Adventist movement was founded by William Miller, an American Baptist preacher, who predicted Jesus' return in 1843. When this didn't happen, he revised his prediction to 1844. This event became known as the \"Great Disappointment.\" However, a small group of believers, led by Ellen G. White (Miller's wife), continued to believe that Jesus would soon return.\n\nEllen G. White had a series of visions and prophetic dreams that guided her understanding of biblical prophecy. She believed that Jesus' second coming was imminent and that it would be preceded by a period of great tribulation, during which God would punish those who refused to repent. This idea was central to the Adventist movement.\n\nThe Watch Tower Society (Jehovah's Witnesses) emerged from this Adventist movement in the late 19th century. Charles Taze Russell, one of the key figures in the development of Jehovah's Witnesses, was influenced by Ellen G. White's teachings and writings. However, he eventually broke away from the Adventist movement and founded his own organization.\n\nIt's important to note that while both movements share some similarities, they have distinct beliefs and practices. The intention behind these predictions and warnings is indeed to encourage people to repent and prepare for Jesus' return, not to strike fear into anyone's heart."}
