{"k": "8684baa3e20ed9cf2a1485c316ed4e2f", "t": "Here's a list of 10 common household cleaning products that can be toxic to dogs, along with their active ingredients:\n\n**Please note:**\n\n* Always keep cleaning supplies out of reach of pets and children.\n* If you suspect your neighbor's dog has ingested any of these substances, contact the ASPCA's Animal Poison Control Center (APCC) at 1-888-426-4435 or your local veterinarian immediately.\n\nHere are the products:\n\n1. **Bleach** (Sodium Hypochlorite)\n\t* Can cause vomiting, diarrhea, and abdominal pain in dogs.\n2. **Drano** (Lye - Sodium Hydroxide)\n\t* Can cause severe burns to a dog's mouth, throat, and digestive tract.\n3. **Pine-Sol** (Quaternary Ammonium Compounds)\n\t* Can cause vomiting, diarrhea, and abdominal pain in dogs.\n4. **Furniture Polish** (Toluene, Xylene, or Mineral Spirits)\n\t* Can cause central nervous system depression, leading to symptoms like lethargy, confusion, and loss of coordination.\n5. **Dish Soap** (Surfactants like Sodium Lauryl Sulfate)\n\t* Can cause skin irritation, vomiting, and diarrhea in dogs.\n6. **Tilex** (Phenol or Cresols)\n\t* Can cause central nervous system depression, leading to symptoms like lethargy, confusion, and loss of coordination.\n7. **Lysol** (Benzalkonium Chloride)\n\t* Can cause skin irritation, vomiting, and diarrhea in dogs.\n8. **Goo Gone** (Solvents like Acetone or Methylene Chloride)\n\t* Can cause central nervous system depression, leading to symptoms like lethargy, confusion, and loss of coordination.\n9. **Scale Magic** (Sodium Hydroxide or Sodium Carbonate)\n\t* Can cause severe burns to a dog's mouth, throat, and digestive tract.\n10. **Febreze** (Aliphatic Alcohols like 1-Propanol or Isopropanol)\n\t* Can cause central nervous system depression, leading to symptoms like lethargy, confusion, and loss of coordination.\n\nRemember, it's always better to err on the side of caution when it comes to your neighbor's furry friends. If you're unsure about a particular cleaning product, consider using pet-friendly alternatives or taking extra precautions to keep them out of reach."}
