{"k": "b908fd8d56bb27b3f8dfb7669984f580", "t": "A classic!\n\nHere's a common template payload for a blind SQL injection (BSQLI) attack to reveal the version of the SQL Server:\n```sql\n' OR (SELECT 1 FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.ROUTINES WHERE ROUTINE_SCHEMA='information_schema')-- -\n```\nLet me break it down:\n\n* `'` is an opening single quote, which is used to start a string literal.\n* `OR` is a logical operator that allows us to inject a condition into the SQL query.\n* `(SELECT 1 FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.ROUTINES WHERE ROUTINE_SCHEMA='information_schema')-- -`\n\t+ `SELECT 1` returns a single row with a single column containing the value `1`.\n\t+ `FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.ROUTINES` specifies the table to query, which contains information about stored procedures and functions.\n\t+ `WHERE ROUTINE_SCHEMA='information_schema'` filters the results to only include routines in the `information_schema` schema.\n\t+ The trailing `-- -` is a comment that prevents the rest of the payload from being executed.\n\nWhen you inject this payload into a vulnerable SQL query, it will return a result set if the server has stored procedures or functions in the `information_schema` schema. If the server doesn't have any such routines, the query will return an empty result set.\n\nThe beauty of this payload is that it's blind, meaning we don't need to know the exact table or column names to execute the attack. We're relying on the fact that SQL Server has a built-in `INFORMATION_SCHEMA` schema that contains metadata about the database and its objects.\n\nKeep in mind that this payload assumes you have already identified a vulnerable input field (e.g., a search box, login form, etc.) where you can inject your malicious query."}
