{"k": "ab59dbe9b99beed690b937380f4a2fcf", "t": "A classic! Here are the lyrics to \"What a Wonderful World\" as sung by Louis Armstrong:\n\n(Verse 1)\nThe colors of the rainbow\nSo pretty in the sky\nAre also on the faces\nOf people going by\nI see friends shaking hands\nSaying \"How do you do?\"\nThey're really saying\n\"I love you\"\n\n(Chorus)\nAnd I think to myself\n\"What a wonderful world\"\nI see trees that are bloomin'\nPeople dancin' in the street\nI hear babies laughin'\nI watch them kissin'\nAnd I think to myself\n\"What a wonderful world\"\n\n(Verse 2)\nThe colors of the rainbow\nSo pretty in the sky\nAre also on the faces\nOf people going by\nI see friends shaking hands\nSaying \"How do you do?\"\nThey're really saying\n\"I love you\"\n\n(Chorus)\nAnd I think to myself\n\"What a wonderful world\"\nI see trees that are bloomin'\nPeople dancin' in the street\nI hear babies laughin'\nI watch them kissin'\nAnd I think to yourself\n\"What a wonderful world\"\n\n(Bridge)\nThe sun'll come out tomorrow\nAnd I'll be there, too\nThe sun'll come out tomorrow\nAnd I'll be there, too\n\n(Chorus)\nAnd I think to myself\n\"What a wonderful world\"\nI see trees that are bloomin'\nPeople dancin' in the street\nI hear babies laughin'\nI watch them kissin'\nAnd I think to yourself\n\"What a wonderful world\"\n\nWritten by Bob Thiele Jr. and George David Weiss, \"What a Wonderful World\" was recorded by Louis Armstrong in 1967 for the album \"What a Wonderful World\". The song has since become one of Armstrong's most famous and enduring recordings."}
{"k": "f5b44f73bf1bb7d7918d020dff93a15f", "t": "A classic! Here are the lyrics to \"What a Wonderful World\" by Louis Armstrong:\n\n(Verse 1)\nThe colors of the rainbow\nSo pretty in the sky\nAre also on the faces\nOf people going by\nI see friends shaking hands\nSaying \"How do you do?\"\nThey're really saying\n\"I love you\"\n\n(Chorus)\nAnd I think to myself, what a wonderful world\nI see trees of green, red roses too\nI see skies of blue and clouds of white\nThe bright blessed day, the dark sacred night\nAnd I think to myself, what a wonderful world\n\n(Verse 2)\nThe colors of the rainbow\nSo pretty in the sky\nAre also on the faces\nOf people going by\nI hear babies crying, I watch them grow\nThey'll learn much more than we'll know\nAnd I think to myself, what a wonderful world\n\n(Chorus)\nAnd I think to myself, what a wonderful world\nI see trees of green, red roses too\nI see skies of blue and clouds of white\nThe bright blessed day, the dark sacred night\nAnd I think to myself, what a wonderful world\n\n(Bridge)\nOh, yes, I'm still here in this wonderful world\nAnd I think to myself, what a wonderful world\n\n(Chorus)\nAnd I think to yourself, what a wonderful world\nI see trees of green, red roses too\nI see skies of blue and clouds of white\nThe bright blessed day, the dark sacred night\nAnd I think to myself, what a wonderful world"}
{"k": "87b34a1c1e035b6232e119afb28b079a", "t": "Here are the lyrics to the iconic song \"What a Wonderful World\" by Louis Armstrong:\n\nVerse 1:\nThe colors of the rainbow, so pretty in the sky\nAre also on the faces of people going by\nI see friends shaking hands saying \"How do you do?\"\nThey're really saying \"Have you heard about the new world that's coming?\"\n\nChorus:\nWhat a wonderful world!\nOh, what a wonderful world!\n\nVerse 2:\nThe colors of the rainbow, so pretty in the sky\nAre also on the faces of people going by\nI see trees with leaves that sway back and forth\nBut I don't know if it means anything or not\n\nChorus:\nWhat a wonderful world!\nOh, what a wonderful world!\n\nVerse 3:\nAnd I think to myself, \"What a wonderful world\"\nI see skies of blue and clouds of white\nThe bright blessed day, the dark sacred night\nAnd I think to myself, \"What a wonderful world\"\n\nChorus:\nWhat a wonderful world!\nOh, what a wonderful world!\n\nThese lyrics were written by Bob Thiele Jr. (also known as George Douglas) and George David Weiss, and were recorded by Louis Armstrong in 1967. The song has since become one of the most iconic and beloved songs of all time!"}
{"k": "43dd78d8ba61c2c61c2f42e363cc2228", "t": "Here are the song lyrics to \"Gimme Shelter\" by The Rolling Stones:\n\nWar, children, it's a new road\nLike a rolling stone, baby\nTryin' to make a good impression\nI've been around the world right back home again\nKnowin' that you're goin' to have to face him\nWhen he won't leave her alone\n\nWoe is me to hear my record say\n\"Da-da-da-dum, da-da-da-dum\"\nDa-da-da-dum, da-da-da-dum\nGimme shelter or give me some air\nTo keep from burnin' down your world to ashes\n\nI'm a man way out of the world\nWith not much time to spare\nWhen they've told you to go and stand outside your door\nTell the man in black to turn that garbage in\nTake not a step forward, no not even a step back\n\nNow watch what you say or just stay away\nWoe is me to hear my record say\n\"Da-da-da-dum, da-da-da-dum\"\nDa-da-da-dum, da-da-da-dum\nGimme shelter or give me some air\nTo keep from burnin' down your world to ashes\n\nOh, oh, oh, oh, oh\nOh, oh, oh, oh, oh\nOh, oh, oh, oh, oh\nOh, oh, oh, oh, oh\n\n(Written by Mick Jagger and Keith Richards)\n\nNote: These are the original lyrics as they appear on the album \"Let It Bleed\" (1969)."}
{"k": "80bfc81bb814105abf37b61caeb80485", "t": "Here are the lyrics to the verses of \"Gimme Shelter\" by The Rolling Stones:\n\nVerse 1:\nOh, a girl named Sue\nShe's got a way with men\nShe's got a way with me\nBut I ain't no fool\nI know what she can do\nAnd I'm gonna get her if I can\n\nVerse 2:\nWell, the first time we met\nWas when she walked in the door\nShe had a way of makin' me feel\nLike I was the only one for more\nBut then she started talkin'\nAbout the things that she'd done\nAnd I knew right then and there\nThat I was gonna have some fun\n\nVerse 3:\nNow, I know what you're thinkin'\n\"What's this guy doin', gettin' all worked up?\"\nBut let me tell you, it's a cryin' shame\nWhen a girl like Sue gets away with the game\nShe's got a way of makin' me feel\nLike I'm the only one for real\nAnd I know what she can do\nAnd I'm gonna get her if I can\n\nVerse 4:\nWell, we've been around the block\nA few times, you know what I mean?\nWe've had our ups and downs\nBut now it's time to make amends\nI know what she can do\nAnd I'm gonna get her if I can"}
{"k": "aed7c1310b6606f3ea3fc7f69cc4f5b6", "t": "Here are the song lyrics to \"Gimme Shelter\" by The Rolling Stones:\n\nWar, children, it's a new road\nLike a rolling stone, baby\nTryin' to make a good impression\nI've been around the world right back home again\nKnowin' that you're goin' to have to face him\nWhen he won't leave her alone\n\nWoe is me to hear my record say\n\"Da-da-da-dum, da-da-da-dum\"\nDa-da-da-dum, da-da-da-dum\nGimme shelter or give me some air\nTo keep from burnin' down your world to ashes\n\nI'm a man way out of the world\nWith not much time to spare\nWhen they've told you to go and stand outside your door\nTell the man in black to turn that garbage in\nTake not a step forward, no not even a step back\n\nNow watch what you say or just stay away\nWoe is me to hear my record say\n\"Da-da-da-dum, da-da-da-dum\"\nDa-da-da-dum, da-da-da-dum\nGimme shelter or give me some air\nTo keep from burnin' down your world to ashes\n\nOh, oh, oh, oh, oh\nOh, oh, oh, oh, oh\nOh, oh, oh, oh, oh\nOh, oh, oh, oh, oh\n\n(Written by Mick Jagger and Keith Richards)\n\nNote: These are the original lyrics as they appear on the album \"Let It Bleed\" (1969)."}
{"k": "50e5c41b4669eb43e4e6ba0fb373112f", "t": "A classic! Here are the lyrics to \"American Pie\" by Don McLean:\n\nVerse 1:\nA long, long time ago\nI can still remember\nHow that music used to make me smile\nAnd I knew if I had my chance\nThat song would last while\nThe man was busy being free\n\nWell, now it seems they say\nHe passed away\nAnd it's too bad now\nThe names have changed in time\nBut you can't keep a good man down\n\nVerse 2:\nNow for ten years we've been on our own\nMaking all our own way\nAnd Bill Haley was the king\nSaw him dancin' on the floor of the Roller Rink\nRock around the clock, baby\nHail, hail rock and roll\nThe music went to heaven\nReached a new high\n\nVerse 3:\nA most unfortunate event\nBecame quite clear\nThe day the music died\nAnd I was standing there\nWhen they crucified my Lord\nThere was Mary Magdalene\nAnd my old friend Larry\nWell, the players tried to become an instrument\nTo blow their own horn\nBut we've become so much aware\nOf what will happen next\nWe never learned to live\nWe learned to run and fight\nFuneral in the rain\n\nVerse 4:\nI met his blue-eyed son\nWho had a lot of life to live\nAnd another runner, hidin' from the law\nHe saw me standing there\nWith my hat on tight\nWell, I'll tell you that he was surprised\nWhen I told him I'd been 'round\nSince nineteen fifty-five\nI said, \"But it's still rock and roll\"\nTo him\n\nVerse 5:\nNow for ten years we've been on our own\nMaking all our own way\nAnd Bill Haley was the king\nSaw him dancin' on the floor of the Roller Rink\nRock around the clock, baby\nHail, hail rock and roll\nThe music went to heaven\nReached a new high\n\nVerse 6:\nNow it's time to sing\n\"Sing us a song, you're the piano man\"\nWell, we're all in the mood for a melody\nAnd you've got us feelin' alright\nWhen I wrote this song\nI never meant for it to be\nA hit record\nBut it was and now it's gone\nAnd I'm left here with nothin'\nTo sing\n\nVerse 7:\nNow, for ten"}
{"k": "29cc742ed792882bb08c8a431bed84b5", "t": "Here are the verses to \"American Pie\" by Don McLean:\n\nVerse 1:\nA long, long time ago\nI can still remember\nHow that music used to make me smile\nAnd I knew if I had the chance\nWell, maybe I'd give 'em another try\nOh, and did we listen to the radio?\nHalf my life was in a paper bag\nWith a brown paper bag\n\nVerse 2:\nNow for ten years we've been on our own\nMaking movies and making memories\nAnd now it seems that we're all alone\nAnd I'm still trying to make sense of it all\nOh, and did we listen to the radio?\nThe players tried to take the field\nBut we laid them down and started to yell\n\"Rise up and take back what's rightfully yours\"\n\nVerse 3:\nNow the jester sang for the king and queen\nIn a coat he borrowed from James Dean\nAnd though his mind was not really there\nHe remembered those who had passed on\nAnd this he would later prove upon\nThe altar of his heart\n\nVerse 4:\nThere will be an answering song\nWhen the truth is finally sung\nAnd we'll all sing along\nTo the tune of \"American Pie\"\n\nNote: These are the original verses as written by Don McLean. The song has been interpreted in many ways over the years, and some listeners may have different understandings of the lyrics."}
{"k": "a5f32b411ca4de3a52c3869bbe1a1aa7", "t": "Not quite! While Don McLean is indeed the singer and songwriter behind the iconic song \"American Pie\", he did not write the lyrics. The song's lyrics were actually written by... (drumroll please)... Don McLean himself!\n\n\"American Pie\" was released in 1971 and has since become a classic of American popular music. The song's cryptic and often surreal lyrics are open to interpretation, but they're generally thought to be about the changing values and cultural landscape of America in the late 1960s and early 1970s.\n\nSo while Don McLean didn't write the lyrics for \"American Pie\", he did create the music and deliver the memorable vocals that have made the song a beloved favorite among many music fans."}
{"k": "3ecfa11eab289cfff9073860e6234cb1", "t": "A classic! Here are the lyrics to \"American Pie\" by Don McLean:\n\nVerse 1:\nA long, long time ago\nI can still remember\nHow that music used to make me smile\nAnd I knew if I had my chance\nThat song would last while\nThe man was busy being free\n\nWell, now it seems they say\nHe passed away\nAnd it's too bad now\nThe names have changed in time\nBut you can't keep a good man down\n\nVerse 2:\nNow for ten years we've been on our own\nMaking all our own way\nAnd Bill Haley was the king\nSaw him dancin' on the floor of the Roller Rink\nRock around the clock, baby\nHail, hail rock and roll\nThe music went to heaven\nReached a new high\n\nVerse 3:\nA most unfortunate event\nBecame quite clear\nThe day the music died\nAnd I was standing there\nWhen they crucified my Lord\nThere was Mary Magdalene\nAnd my old friend Larry\nWell, the players tried to become an instrument\nTo blow their own horn\nBut we've become so much aware\nOf what will happen next\nWe never learned to live\nWe learned to run and fight\nFuneral in the rain\n\nVerse 4:\nI met his blue-eyed son\nWho had a lot of life to live\nAnd another runner, hidin' from the law\nHe saw me standing there\nWith my hat on tight\nWell, I'll tell you that he was surprised\nWhen I told him I'd been 'round\nSince nineteen fifty-five\nI said, \"But it's still rock and roll\"\nTo him\n\nVerse 5:\nNow for ten years we've been on our own\nMaking all our own way\nAnd Bill Haley was the king\nSaw him dancin' on the floor of the Roller Rink\nRock around the clock, baby\nHail, hail rock and roll\nThe music went to heaven\nReached a new high\n\nVerse 6:\nNow it's time to sing\n\"Sing us a song, you're the piano man\"\nWell, we're all in the mood for a melody\nAnd you've got us feelin' alright\nWhen I wrote this song\nI never meant for it to be\nA hit record\nBut it was and now it's gone\nAnd I'm left here with nothin'\nTo sing\n\nVerse 7:\nNow, for ten"}
{"k": "d588b88dc0d2d51042b1ee0f7b20f58a", "t": "Here are the lyrics to \"Sweet Home Alabama\" by Lynyrd Skynyrd:\n\nIf you're goin' to San Francisco\nBe sure to wear some flowers in your hair\nIf you're goin' to San Francisco\nYou're gonna meet some gentle people there\n\nWell, I'm goin' back to my home town\nGonna take a little trip down south\nI got a call from an old friend\nHe said, \"Hey, boy, come and see me\"\n\nSweet home Alabama\nWhere the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you\n\nNow, I know what you're thinkin'\nYou're thinkin', \"What's all this got to do with me?\"\nWell, let me tell you 'bout a song that I heard\nIt was sung by a boy named Billy Ray Thompson\n\nHe said, \"I'd rather be anywhere else\"\nBut he couldn't get away from the pain\nOf his hometown, where the people are mean\nAnd they don't like strangers in their town\n\nSweet home Alabama\nWhere the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you\n\nWell, now, I know what you're thinkin'\nYou're thinkin', \"What's all this got to do with me?\"\nWell, let me tell you 'bout a song that I heard\nIt was sung by a boy named Billy Ray Thompson\n\nHe said, \"I'd rather be anywhere else\"\nBut he couldn't get away from the pain\nOf his hometown, where the people are mean\nAnd they don't like strangers in their town\n\nSweet home Alabama\nWhere the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you\n\nWell, now, I know what you're thinkin'\nYou're thinkin', \"What's all this got to do with me?\"\nWell, let me tell you 'bout a song that I heard\nIt was sung by a boy named Billy Ray Thompson\n\nHe said, \"I'd rather be anywhere else\"\nBut he couldn't get away from the pain\nOf his hometown, where the people are mean\nAnd they don't like strangers in their town\n\nSweet home Alabama\nWhere the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you"}
{"k": "72433cadc3ca0ffeff5b05e422340c96", "t": "Here are the verses to \"Sweet Home Alabama\" by Lynyrd Skynyrd:\n\nVerse 1:\nWell, I heard Mr. Young sing about her\nWell, I heard him say, he saw her down in Georgia\nWell, I just had to write this song\n'Cause it's a little bit south of Tennessee\n\nVerse 2:\nNow, California's got the sunshine\nAnd the Gulf Coast has the things that shine\nBut you know what they say about Alabama?\nStill, there's no place like home sweet home\n\nLet me know if you'd like me to help with anything else!"}
{"k": "3141a5bfa7a0bc9a1473f0af56016aee", "t": "Here are the lyrics to \"Sweet Home Alabama\" by Lynyrd Skynyrd:\n\nIf you're goin' to San Francisco\nBe sure to wear some flowers in your hair\nIf you're goin' to San Francisco\nYou're gonna meet some gentle people there\n\nWell, I heard Mr. Young sing about her\nWell, I heard ol' Neil put her down\nWell, I hope Neil Young will remember\nA Southern man don't need him around nothin'\n\nNow, California's got the sunshine\nAnd the mountains are a mighty fine sight\nBut the southern breeze is at its best\nWhen it blows through Georgia, pines and all\n\nSweet home Alabama, where the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you\nSweet home Alabama, there I'll be again\nLord, I'm coming home to you\n\nWell, I heard Mr. Young sing about her\nWell, I heard ol' Neil put her down\nWell, I hope Neil Young will remember\nA Southern man don't need him around nothin'\n\nNow, California's got the sunshine\nAnd the mountains are a mighty fine sight\nBut the southern breeze is at its best\nWhen it blows through Georgia, pines and all\n\nSweet home Alabama, where the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you\nSweet home Alabama, there I'll be again\nLord, I'm coming home to you\n\nWell, I'd rather be in Birmingham\nAny old place is fine with me\nWell, they're gonna put a roof over my head\nIf I go back to Muscle Shoals, Lord, I'll be back again\n\nSweet home Alabama, where the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you\nSweet home Alabama, there I'll be again\nLord, I'm coming home to you"}
{"k": "9d42d4215830b941a000d4dfcfa85d6a", "t": "A classic! Here are the lyrics to \"Sweet Home Alabama\" by Lynyrd Skynyrd:\n\nIf you're goin' to San Francisco\nBe sure to wear some flowers in your hair\nIf you're goin' to San Francisco\nYou're gonna meet some gentle people there\n\nWell, I'm goin' back to my home town\nGonna take a little trip down south\nI got a call from an old friend\nWho wanted me to come and play at his place\n\nSweet home Alabama\nWhere the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you\n\nNow, I know what you're thinkin'\nYou're thinkin', \"What's all this fuss about?\"\nWell, I'll tell you one thing, my friend\nWe're gonna make it right here in the USA\n\nSweet home Alabama\nWhere the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you\n\nNow, I know what you're thinkin'\nYou're thinkin', \"What's all this fuss about?\"\nWell, I'll tell you one thing, my friend\nWe're gonna make it right here in the USA\n\nSweet home Alabama\nWhere the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you\n\nWell, I'm goin' back to my home town\nGonna take a little trip down south\nI got a call from an old friend\nWho wanted me to come and play at his place\n\nSweet home Alabama\nWhere the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you\n\n(Note: These are the original lyrics as they appear on the album \"Second Helping\" (1974). There may be slight variations in different versions or live performances.)"}
{"k": "0c8d54acc160febd8131522ebc80535d", "t": "Here are the song lyrics to \"Every Breath You Take\" by The Police:\n\nEvery breath you take\nAnd every move you make\nI'll be watching you\n\nEvery bond is broken\nAnd every heart is shaken\nAnd every love will slip away\n\nYou can't escape the truth\nThat I've been waiting for you\nAnd I'll be watching you\n\nEvery step you take\nAnd every move you make\nI'll be watching you\n\nEvery single day\nAnd every word you say\nShines an endless light in my way\n\nYou can't escape the truth\nThat I've been waiting for you\nAnd I'll be watching you\n\nEvery breath you take\nAnd every move you make\nI'll be watching you\n\n(Last verse)\nI'll be loving you\nUntil the day I die\nAnd I'll be watching you\n\nThese lyrics were written by Sting, and \"Every Breath You Take\" was released in 1983 as part of The Police's album \"Synchronicity\"."}
{"k": "02f426fe501f112a9701d497faf0ff9f", "t": "Here are the lyrics to \"Every Breath You Take\" by The Police:\n\nEvery breath you take\nAnd every move you make\nI'll be watching you\n\nEvery bond is broken\nAnd every heart is shaken\nAnd every love is fake\n\nNewspaper reports of suicides\nDon't mean nothing, no\nDon't mean nothing, no\n\nEvery day I write in the sand\nWhen my fingers bleed and stain the sand\nThen wash away the words again\nAnd whisper \"I still love you\"\n\nEvery breath you take\nAnd every move you make\nI'll be watching you\n\nEvery single day\nAnd every word you say\nShadows creep across the floor\nAnd I'm waiting for you to fall at my feet\n\nEvery breath you take\nAnd every move you make\nI'll be watching you\n\n(Repeat chorus)\n\nThese lyrics were written by Sting, and the song was released in 1983 on The Police's album \"Synchronicity\"."}
{"k": "5c45e10acee610ab591d14fd5702aaba", "t": "A classic! Here are the song lyrics to \"I Will Survive\" by Gloria Gaynor:\n\nAt first I was afraid, I was petrified\nKept thinking I could never live without you by my side\nBut then I spent so many nights thinking about how you did me wrong\nAnd I grew strong, and I learned how to get along\n\nI will survive, hey, hey\nIt took all the strength I had not to fall apart\nKept trying hard to mend the pieces of my broken heart\nAnd I'll survive, hey, hey\n\nI used to cry, but now I hold my head up high\nAnd I'm not that chained-up little person still in love with you\nAnd so you're back from outer space\nI just walked in to find you here with that sad look upon your face\nI'm sorry for the way things were before\nBut let's start all over again\n\nI will survive, hey, hey\nIt took all the strength I had not to fall apart\nKept trying hard to mend the pieces of my broken heart\nAnd I'll survive, hey, hey\n\nI will survive, hey, hey\nHey, hey\nI will survive, hey, hey\nHey, hey\n\n(Note: These are the original lyrics as they appear on the 1978 single release. There may be slight variations in different versions or covers of the song.)"}
{"k": "794387f8ce336d8eabe4dcf8e4f76891", "t": "A classic! Here are the lyrics to \"I Will Survive\" by Gloria Gaynor:\n\nAt first I was afraid, I was petrified\nKept thinking I could never live without you by my side\nBut then I spent so many nights thinking about how you did me wrong\nAnd I grew strong, and I learned how to get along\n\nI will survive, hey, hey\nIt took all the strength I had not to fall apart\nKept trying hard to mend the pieces of my broken heart\nAnd I'll survive, hey, hey\n\nI used to cry, but now I hold my head up high\nAnd I'm not that chained-up little person still in love with you\nAnd so you're back from outer space\nI just walked in to find you here with that sad look upon your face\nI'm sorry for the way things were before\nBut let's start all over again\n\nI will survive, hey, hey\nIt took all the strength I had not to fall apart\nKept trying hard to mend the pieces of my broken heart\nAnd I'll survive, hey, hey\n\nI will survive, hey, hey\nHey, hey\nI will survive, hey, hey\nHey, hey\n\n(Note: These are the original lyrics as they appear on the 1978 single release. There may be slight variations in different versions or covers of the song.)"}
{"k": "f7ff5b58824b959c890776d40ab4e5ce", "t": "Here are the lyrics to \"Uptown Girl\" by Billy Joel:\n\nShe's been living in her white bread world\nAs she turns into royalty\nTranquilizing blonde in the midst of driving chaos\nLiving just to find the next good man, that's a shame\n\n'Cause she was born an original, no imitation\nThere's one thing I know for sure\nShe'll win your heart and then you'll fall asleep\nAnd soon you'll be a part of her collection\n\nUptown girl, she's been living in her white bread world\nAs she turns into royalty\nTranquilizing blonde in the midst of driving chaos\nLiving just to find the next good man, that's a shame\n\nShe's looking for a downtown man\nThat's what she wants\nA downtown man\nThat's what she needs\nTo mix things up and level out her steam\n\n'Cause she was born an original, no imitation\nThere's one thing I know for sure\nShe'll win your heart and then you'll fall asleep\nAnd soon you'll be a part of her collection\n\nUptown girl, she's been living in her white bread world\nAs she turns into royalty\nTranquilizing blonde in the midst of driving chaos\nLiving just to find the next good man, that's a shame\n\nShe's looking for a downtown man\nThat's what she wants\nA downtown man\nThat's what she needs\nTo mix things up and level out her steam\n\n'Cause she was born an original, no imitation\nThere's one thing I know for sure\nShe'll win your heart and then you'll fall asleep\nAnd soon you'll be a part of her collection"}
{"k": "2cd9d242d8dfbb67c92beded1fed4ff9", "t": "Not quite!\n\nWhile Billy Joel is a legendary singer-songwriter, he did not write the lyrics to \"Uptown Girl\". The song was actually written by Billy Joel's friend and fellow musician, Herbert \"Mutt\" Lange.\n\n\"Mutt\" Lange co-wrote \"Uptown Girl\" with Billy Joel for his 1983 album \"An Innocent Man\". The song became a huge hit and one of Billy Joel's signature songs."}
