{"k": "c359cec4050c597e1969e566223259d4", "t": "A classic! Here are the lyrics to the verses of \"Pinball Wizard\" by The Who:\n\n**Verse 1**\nI can see you standing there\nIn your pinball machine, staring at the lights\nYou're a pinball wizard, zooming like lightning\nZooming through the flippers with all your might\n\n**Verse 2**\nYou're a pinball wizard, zooming like lightning\nZooming through the flippers with all your might\nYou're a pinball wizard, zooming like lightning\nZooming through the flippers with all your might\n\n**Verse 3**\nI can see you standing there\nIn your pinball machine, staring at the lights\nYou're a pinball wizard, zooming like lightning\nZooming through the flippers with all your might\n\nThese verses are repeated throughout the song, with some slight variations and additions in the choruses. The song is known for its catchy melody, energetic instrumentation, and humorous lyrics that poke fun at the obsession with pinball machines."}
{"k": "739ca559b8995ebb08def976a303c251", "t": "A classic! Here are the lyrics to \"The Pinball Wizard\" from The Who's iconic rock opera, Tommy:\n\n[Verse 1 - The Narrator]\nThere's a boy with a pinball machine\nIn his bedroom, it's all he can be\nHe's got a dream, and that dream is to win\nAnd play the game until the end of time\n\n[Chorus]\nThe Pinball Wizard, he's the one\nWho'll help you win, if you're having fun\nHe's the master of the silver ball\nThe Pinball Wizard, standing tall!\n\n[Verse 2 - The Narrator]\nHe's got a score to settle with his friend\nA game of skill that will never end\nHe's got a plan, and that plan is to win\nAnd prove he's the greatest pinball whiz within\n\n[Chorus]\nThe Pinball Wizard, he's the one\nWho'll help you win, if you're having fun\nHe's the master of the silver ball\nThe Pinball Wizard, standing tall!\n\n[Bridge - The Pinball Wizard]\nI'm the Pinball Wizard, I've got a tale to tell\nOf flippers and bumpers, and the thrill of the bell\nI'll help you win, if you're feeling blue\nJust give me a quarter, and I'll see you through\n\n[Verse 3 - The Narrator]\nHe's got a dream, and that dream is to win\nAnd play the game until the end of time\nThe Pinball Wizard, he's the one\nWho'll help you win, if you're having fun\n\n[Chorus]\nThe Pinball Wizard, he's the one\nWho'll help you win, if you're having fun\nHe's the master of the silver ball\nThe Pinball Wizard, standing tall!\n\nThese lyrics are a testament to The Who's storytelling ability and their knack for crafting memorable characters. \"The Pinball Wizard\" is just one of many iconic songs from Tommy, which has become a rock 'n' roll classic."}
{"k": "40b7f7c271bddd284b90d0c4adbcb2ae", "t": "A classic! Here are the song lyrics to \"Pinball Wizard\" by The Who:\n\n**Verse 1**\nI can see you're a pinball wizard\nZooming in and out, zooming in and out\nYou're a pinball wizard, zooming in and out\nAnd you never lose your spin\n\n**Chorus**\nPinball wizard, pinball wizard\nHe's the king of the game\nPinball wizard, pinball wizard\nHe's the one to claim\n\n**Verse 2**\nI can see you're a pinball wizard\nZooming in and out, zooming in and out\nYou're a pinball wizard, zooming in and out\nAnd you never lose your spin\n\n**Chorus**\nPinball wizard, pinball wizard\nHe's the king of the game\nPinball wizard, pinball wizard\nHe's the one to claim\n\n**Bridge**\nI'm not sure what I'd do if I were you\nBut I know how I'll get through\nJust stay calm and play it cool\nAnd don't let your emotions rule\n\n**Chorus**\nPinball wizard, pinball wizard\nHe's the king of the game\nPinball wizard, pinball wizard\nHe's the one to claim\n\n**Outro**\nI can see you're a pinball wizard\nZooming in and out, zooming in and out\nYou're a pinball wizard, zooming in and out\nAnd you never lose your spin\n\nThese lyrics are from the 1969 rock opera album \"Tommy\" by The Who."}
{"k": "7928846e0103eaa3de2ce1f22581acfd", "t": "Here are the lyrics to \"Maggie May\" by Rod Stewart:\n\nWell, she came and laid her head on my lap\nAnd I knew that it was only a matter of time\nBefore I fell for her charms\nAnd I knew that it was only a matter of time\nBefore I fell for her charms\n\nNow, Maggie May has got one fault\nShe's got the very best of them all\nAnd she'll tell you when she's thirty-four\nThat she's had twenty years of boredom and more\n\nWell, she won't find what she's looking for\nAnd maybe we can make it together\nStay with me, stay with me\nAnd we'll be fine, just you wait and see\n\nNow, Maggie May has got one fault\nShe's got the very best of them all\nAnd she'll tell you when she's thirty-four\nThat she's had twenty years of boredom and more\n\nWell, she won't find what she's looking for\nAnd maybe we can make it together\nStay with me, stay with me\nAnd we'll be fine, just you wait and see\n\nNow, Maggie May has got one fault\nShe's got the very best of them all\nAnd she'll tell you when she's thirty-four\nThat she's had twenty years of boredom and more\n\nWell, she won't find what she's looking for\nAnd maybe we can make it together\nStay with me, stay with me\nAnd we'll be fine, just you wait and see"}
{"k": "c634b4e41f3a0b68adfb7bc4792321c6", "t": "Here are the lyrics to the verses of \"Maggie May\" by Rod Stewart:\n\nVerse 1:\nNow you need some clean air\nHanging around, staring at the floor\nAnd I was thinking that maybe we could make a deal\nWhy don't you relax and take it easy?\n\nVerse 2:\nYou've got your mother's wisdom\nAnd your father's sense of humor\nBut I'm not sure about my own\nI'm still trying to find out who I am\n\nLet me know if you'd like me to help with the chorus or any other parts of the song!"}
{"k": "b44967baee8122d0ec7d06aaa3234d3d", "t": "Not quite!\n\nWhile Rod Stewart is indeed the singer and performer of the iconic song \"Maggie May\", he did not write the lyrics. The song was actually written by Martin Quittenton, a British musician and songwriter.\n\nQuittenton wrote the song in 1970, and it was released as a single from Rod Stewart's album \"Every Picture Tells a Story\" in 1971. The song became one of Stewart's signature hits and a classic rock staple.\n\nSo while Rod Stewart may have brought the song to life with his distinctive vocals, Martin Quittenton is the one who penned the lyrics that make \"Maggie May\" such an enduring favorite!"}
{"k": "3d13b925ce227efda0a95633e7f22da3", "t": "A classic! Here are the lyrics to \"Maggie May\" by Rod Stewart:\n\nWell, she came and stood in my garden,\nWith flowers in her hair.\nJust a runaway, but I knew right then\nThat I would never be the same again.\n\nNow, Maggie May has got it bad,\nThink she's lost her grip on reality.\nShe's gone to the doctor, and what did he say?\n\"Leave your worries behind, dear.\"\n\nWell, she said, \"I've got your little boy\nTo think about now.\"\nAnd I said, \"But what about us?\"\nJust a runaway, but I knew right then\nThat I would never be the same again.\n\nNow, Maggie May has got it bad,\nThink she's lost her grip on reality.\nShe's gone to the doctor, and what did he say?\n\"Leave your worries behind, dear.\"\n\nWell, we'll have some fun now,\nLet the good times roll.\nWe will make our own way,\nAnd we won't be told.\n\nNow, Maggie May has got it bad,\nThink she's lost her grip on reality.\nShe's gone to the doctor, and what did he say?\n\"Leave your worries behind, dear.\"\n\nWell, I'll never let you go,\nMaggie May, oh, Maggie May.\nYou can check out any time you like,\nBut you can never leave.\n\nThese lyrics are from the original 1971 album version of \"Maggie May\"."}
