{"k": "cec543f4afdb8f3f8e93e3e02d86408b", "t": "Here are the song lyrics for \"Born to Run\" by Bruce Springsteen:\n\nIn the day we sweat it out on the streets of a runaway American dream\nSo tired of coming up short, still trying to make ends meet\nAnd you know it's gonna be a long hard road to travel\nBut if I can just make that one last stop\n\nWell, they're ridin' in my car\nWith the radio on and the moon shinin' bright\nWe made a fire escape, we took to the highway\nLooked for a place to crash, where we could rest our bones\n\nAnd you know it's gonna be a long hard road to travel\nBut if I can just make that one last stop\n\nTramps like us, baby we were born to run\nBorn to run\nBorn to run\n\nWe're gonna take it to the highway\nSee what stumbles are waiting there for us\nWe'll take it to the highway\nSee what stumbles are waiting there for us\n\nAnd you know it's gonna be a long hard road to travel\nBut if I can just make that one last stop\n\nTramps like us, baby we were born to run\nBorn to run\nBorn to run\n\nWell, they're ridin' in my car\nWith the radio on and the moon shinin' bright\nWe made a fire escape, we took to the highway\nLooked for a place to crash, where we could rest our bones\n\nAnd you know it's gonna be a long hard road to travel\nBut if I can just make that one last stop\n\nTramps like us, baby we were born to run\nBorn to run\nBorn to run"}
{"k": "537b866e41060b55d1ebb04e2a9fc453", "t": "Not quite!\n\nWhile Bruce Springsteen is indeed the legendary musician behind the iconic song \"Born to Run,\" he didn't write the lyrics. The actual lyricist of \"Born to Run\" is... (drumroll please)... Bruce Springsteen himself!\n\nThat's right, Springsteen wrote the lyrics and music for this classic hit, which was released in 1975 on his album \"Born to Run.\" The song has since become one of his most beloved and enduring songs, and a staple of rock 'n' roll.\n\nSo while it's true that Springsteen didn't write the lyrics alone (he often collaborates with others), he is indeed the primary lyricist behind this iconic anthem."}
{"k": "795734ec8573a7788f01057955f06adb", "t": "Here are the song lyrics to \"Ain't No Mountain High Enough\" by Diana Ross:\n\nVerse 1:\nBaby, baby, I'm amazed and confused\nLookin' at you, lookin' like a king\nWhat's your name, what's your game?\nYou're lookin' like a star, but you ain't got far\n\nChorus:\nAin't no mountain high enough\nAin't no valley low enough\nAin't no river wide enough\nTo keep my love from gettin' to you\n\nVerse 2:\nIf you need me, call me, don't hesitate, baby\nI'll be there on your doorstep, in a hurry 'fore you even place the call\nYou don't have to tell me why, just let me know how and when\nTo come to you, baby, honey, I'm gonna get to you\n\nChorus:\nAin't no mountain high enough\nAin't no valley low enough\nAin't no river wide enough\nTo keep my love from gettin' to you\n\nBridge:\nI made a vow, I'll make it sound\nIn the morning light, or in the evening dew\nI'll be there, baby, honey, don't you know\nI'll be there, baby, honey, ain't no mountain high enough\n\nChorus:\nAin't no mountain high enough\nAin't no valley low enough\nAin\u2019t no river wide enough\nTo keep my love from gettin' to you"}
{"k": "7b82b67b3da52a4268f8730d4518f430", "t": "I apologize, but it seems you've provided incorrect information. There is no song called \"Ain't No Hill Too High\" by Diana Ross.\n\nHowever, I can suggest some popular songs by Diana Ross that might fit your request:\n\n* \"Ain't Nobody Better Than You\" (from the album \"Eaten Alive\", 1985)\n* \"Ain't No Mountain High Enough\" (a duet with Marvin Gaye, from the album \"Together\", 1970)\n\nIf you'd like, I can help you compose verses for one of these songs or even create a new song inspired by Diana Ross's style. Just let me know!"}
{"k": "13a610c88c7d4c7cef9ef49ba340738a", "t": "Not quite!\n\nWhile Diana Ross did record and popularize the song \"Ain't No Mountain High Enough\", she didn't actually generate the lyrics. The song was written by Nickolas Ashford and Valerie Simpson, who were a husband-and-wife songwriting team.\n\nThe song was originally recorded in 1966 by Marvin Gaye and Tammi Terrell, with Ashford and Simpson providing the vocals. It became a huge hit for the duo, reaching number eight on the Billboard Hot 100 chart.\n\nDiana Ross did record her own version of \"Ain't No Mountain High Enough\" in 1970, which was released as a single from the album \"Diana Ross\". Her version is arguably more well-known than the original, and it's considered one of her signature songs."}
{"k": "32022c259d938a4a6490919b6532becf", "t": "Here are the song lyrics to \"Ain't No Mountain High Enough\" by Diana Ross:\n\nVerse 1:\nBaby, baby, I'm amazed and confused\nLookin' at you, lookin' like a king\nWhat's your name, what's your game?\nYou're lookin' like a star, but you ain't got far\n\nChorus:\nAin't no mountain high enough\nAin't no valley low enough\nAin't no river wide enough\nTo keep my love from gettin' to you\n\nVerse 2:\nIf you need me, call me, don't hesitate, baby\nI'll be there on your doorstep, in a hurry 'fore you even place the call\nYou don't have to tell me why, just let me know how and when\nTo come to you, baby, honey, I'm gonna get to you\n\nChorus:\nAin't no mountain high enough\nAin't no valley low enough\nAin't no river wide enough\nTo keep my love from gettin' to you\n\nBridge:\nI made a vow, I'll make it sound\nIn the morning light, or in the evening dew\nI'll be there, baby, honey, don't you know\nI'll be there, baby, honey, ain't no mountain high enough\n\nChorus:\nAin't no mountain high enough\nAin't no valley low enough\nAin\u2019t no river wide enough\nTo keep my love from gettin' to you"}
{"k": "6e703687dcc1357fad5cb95a7a4063bb", "t": "Here are the lyrics to \"Piano Man\" by Billy Joel:\n\nIt's nine o'clock on a Saturday\nThe regular crowd shuffles in\nThere's an old man sitting next to me\nMakin' love to his tonic and his gin\nHe says, \"Son, can you play me a memory?\nI'm not really sure how it goes\nBut it's been comin' for me, oh, oh, oh\"\n\nLa la la la la la la la la la la la\n\nWell, we're all in the mood for a melody\nAnd you've got those pretty, blue eyes\nAnd a voice that is so soothing to my soul\nAnd once you know you can't go back\nWe always did feel the same\nWe just happened to miss each other's name\n\nLa la la la la la la la la la la la\n\nHe says, \"Bill, I believe this is killing me\"\nAs the smile ran away from his face\n\"Well, I'm sure that I could be a movie star\nIf I could get out of this place\"\n\nLa la la la la la la la la la la la\n\nNow Paul is a real estate novelist\nWho never had time for a wife\nAnd he's talkin' with Davida\nBryant who just got settled and he's feeling alright\nHe's talking with Karla, and she keeps giving him the eye\nAnd they're both not really listening to each other\nThey're just happy to be alive\n\nLa la la la la la la la la la la la\n\nIt seems like years since it's been here\nThere's a lot of people talkin' 'bout the pension plan changes\nAll the jazzmen come to hear him play\nHe says, \"Bill, why don't we just have a ball?\"\nAnd then he looks at his watch and says, \"I hope I'm not late\"\nFor that meeting of the Sons of Neptune\n\nLa la la la la la la la la la la la\n\nWell, we're all in the mood for a melody\nAnd you've got those pretty, blue eyes\nAnd a voice that is so soothing to my soul\nAnd once you know you can't go back\nWe always did feel the same\nWe just happened to miss each other's name\n\nLa la la la la la la la la la la la\n\nOh, and there's some guy here who claims he invented E=mc\u00b2\nAnd they're all gonna kiss his feet\n'Cause he's"}
{"k": "8c645e40b9acde46a9e20062e5f1750c", "t": "Not quite! While Billy Joel is indeed the composer and performer of the iconic song \"Piano Man,\" he did not write the lyrics. The lyrics were actually written by Joel himself, along with his friend and collaborator, Al Cleveland.\n\nThe song was released in 1973 on Joel's album \"Piano Man\" (which shares its title with the song), and it has since become one of Joel's most famous and enduring songs."}
{"k": "930ce171a1b06046c669559bd625d592", "t": "Here are the song lyrics to \"Piano Man\" by Billy Joel:\n\nIt's nine o'clock on a Saturday\nThe regular crowd shuffles in\nThere's an old man sitting next to me\nMakin' love to his tonic and his gin\nHe says, \"Son, can you play me a memory?\nI'm not really sure how it goes\nBut it's been comin' for me, oh, oh, oh\"\n\nLa la la la la la la la la la la la\n\nWell, we're all in the mood for a melody\nAnd you've got those pretty, blue eyes\nAnd a voice that is so soothing to my soul\nAnd once you know you can't go back\nWe always did feel the same\nWe just happened to miss each other's name\n\nLa la la la la la la la la la la la\n\nHe says, \"Bill, I believe this is killing me\"\nAs the smile crept up on his face\n\"Well, I'm sure that I could be a movie star\nIf I could get out of this place\"\n\nLa la la la la la la la la la la la\n\nNow Paul is a real estate novelist\nWho never had time for a wife\nAnd he's talkin' with Davida\nBryant and the waitress, and the bartender too\nAnd the cops just left 'em alone\n'Cause nobody else was in the mood\nI am \"the piano man\"\n\nLa la la la la la la la la la la la\n\nIt seems like years since it's been here\nThere's a lot of people talkin'\nBut there's no one listenin'\nAnd the phone's ringing, but I'm not home\nAnd the folks who run this place are so unkind\nAnd they're always sayin', \"Son, you gotta make a stand\"\nAnd I am \"the piano man\"\n\nLa la la la la la la la la la la la\n\nWell, we're all in the mood for a melody\nAnd you've got those pretty, blue eyes\nAnd a voice that is so soothing to my soul\nAnd once you know you can't go back\nWe always did feel the same\nWe just happened to miss each other's name\n\nLa la la la la la la la la la la la"}
{"k": "1586a6a71bc272e4400acfaea1a48877", "t": "Here are the song lyrics to \"Under the Bridge\" by Red Hot Chili Peppers:\n\nSometimes I feel like I don't have a partner\nSometimes I feel like my only friend\nIs the city I live in, the city of angels\nLonely as I am, together we cry\n\nI drive on her streets 'cause she's my companion\nI walk these streets 'cause they know who I am\nShe sees my good deeds and she kisses me windy\nShe takes me away to that place where everything is a song\n\nAnd I sing along\nYou're all I've got till I get to the other side\nSo you go on ahead while I turn back around\n'Cause I'll be loving again, I'll be loving again\n\nI don't ever want to feel like I did that day\nTake me to the place I love\nTake me all the way\nUnder the bridge\n\nAnd I sing along\nYou're all I've got till I get to the other side\nSo you go on ahead while I turn back around\n'Cause I'll be loving again, I'll be loving again\n\nI don't ever want to feel like I did that day\nTake me to the place I love\nTake me all the way\nUnder the bridge\n\nSometimes I feel like I don't have a partner\nSometimes I feel like my only friend\nIs the city I live in, the city of angels\nLonely as I am, together we cry\n\nAnd I sing along\nYou're all I've got till I get to the other side\nSo you go on ahead while I turn back around\n'Cause I'll be loving again, I'll be loving again\n\nI don't ever want to feel like I did that day\nTake me to the place I love\nTake me all the way\nUnder the bridge"}
{"k": "7d0df99fe8af6e4da2f263990a3eb56e", "t": "Here are the lyrics to \"Under the Bridge\" by Red Hot Chili Peppers:\n\nSometimes I feel like I don't have a partner\nSometimes I feel like my only friend\nIs the city I live in, the city of angels\nLonely as I am, together we cry\n\nI drive on her streets 'cause she's my companion\nI walk these streets 'cause they know who I am\nShe sees my good deeds and she kisses me windy\nShe takes me in and understands\n\nAnd makes me feel like I'm part of the band\nWhere Fluff is the leading man\nOh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh\n\nSometimes I feel like I don't have a place\nSometimes I feel like my only friend\nIs the city I live in, the city of angels\nLonely as I am, together we cry\n\nI drive on her streets 'cause she's my companion\nI walk these streets 'cause they know who I am\nShe sees my good deeds and she kisses me windy\nShe takes me in and understands\n\nAnd makes me feel like I'm part of the band\nWhere Fluff is the leading man\nOh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh\n\nNow I can see that that bridge\nIs not just a bridge to nowhere\nI've got my friends by my side\nAnd we'll take it one step at a time\n\nSometimes I feel like I don't have a partner\nSometimes I feel like my only friend\nIs the city I live in, the city of angels\nLonely as I am, together we cry\n\nOh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh\n\n(Note: These are the original lyrics as they appear on the album \"Blood Sugar Sex Magik\". There may be slight variations in different versions or live performances.)"}
