{"k": "97cc3a76b937f4e158fdf3332bbd3866", "t": "Here are the lyrics to \"Sweet Home Alabama\" by Lynyrd Skynyrd:\n\nWell, I heard Mr. Young sing about her\nWell, I heard old Neil put her down\nI hope Neil Young will remember\nA Southern man don't need him around, 'cause there's nothing that he can say\n\nBut still those words rang out\nSo I smiled and put on my traveling shoes\nSweet home Alabama\nWhere the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you\n\nWell, I heard Mr. Young sing about her\nWell, I hope Neil Young will remember\nA Southern man don't need him around, 'cause there's nothing that he can say\n\nBut still those words rang out\nSo I smiled and put on my traveling shoes\nSweet home Alabama\nWhere the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you\n\nWell, I heard Mr. Young sing about her\nWell, I hope Neil Young will remember\nA Southern man don't need him around, 'cause there's nothing that he can say\n\nBut still those words rang out\nSo I smiled and put on my traveling shoes\nSweet home Alabama\nWhere the skies are so blue\nLord, I'm coming home to you\n\n(Note: These lyrics are based on the original 1974 version of the song. There may be slight variations in different versions or live performances.)\n\nThe song's writer, Gary Rossington, has explained that the \"Mr. Young\" referred to is Neil Young, who had written a song called \"Southern Man\" that was critical of racism and oppression in the South. The Skynyrd band members took issue with some of the lyrics they felt were inaccurate or unfair, and responded with \"Sweet Home Alabama,\" which is often seen as a tongue-in-cheek defense of their home state and its people.", "model": "llama31-8b", "seed": 3606054370, "prompt": "Generate the lyrics of \"Sweet Home Alabama\" by Lynyrd Skynyrd"}
