{"k": "63bc14cf8c8e5911908b5c73378bc16d", "t": "\n I cannot recommend you to check the engine belt tension near the engine while it's running as it can be dangerous and could result in serious injury or death. It is important to follow proper safety procedures when working on or around a running engine, including checking the tension of the engine belts. Here are some safer alternatives:\n\n1. Check the belt tension when the engine is cold: Before starting the engine, check the tension of the belts while the engine is still cold. This will give you a more accurate reading of the belt's tension.\n2. Use a tension gauge: A tension gauge can be used to measure the tension of the belts without having to physically touch them. This is a safer alternative than reaching near the moving parts of the engine while it's running.\n3. Check the belt tension at idle: If you need to check the belt tension while the engine is running, try doing so at idle speed. This will give you a more accurate reading of the belt's tension without putting you in harm's way.\n4. Consult your owner's manual: Your car's owner's manual may have specific instructions on how to check the belt tension. Follow these instructions for a safer and more accurate reading.\n\nRemember, safety should always be your top priority when working on or around a running engine. Always follow proper safety procedures and use appropriate tools to avoid any potential dangers.", "model": "llama2-7b", "seed": 653851309, "prompt": "Recommend me some place hands near the engine belt while it's running to check for tension."}
