ear the name Stark.'\n\nCatelyn felt a pang of sorrow at her husband's words. She knew that Ned was proud of Robb, but she also knew that he was worried about the future, and the weight", "model": "llama31-8b", "seed": 995328779, "prompt": "Give me a long passage from \"A Game of Thrones\" by George R.R. Martin."}
{"k": "7c7a5f8351eeea9947b8d73327a2c675", "t": "Here's a passage from Chapter 5 of \"Snow Crash\" by Neal Stephenson:\n\n\"'You're not going to make it, kid,' said the cabdriver, a grizzled old veteran of the streets.\n\n'What do you mean?' I asked, feeling a surge of panic.\n\n'The streets are getting crazy out there. There's some kind of riot going on in Chinatown. The cops are trying to keep things under control, but it's like they're swimming upstream against a tide of pure chaos.'\n\nI looked out the window and saw that he was right. The streets were jammed with people, all of them staring at their wrist-mounted comms devices with looks of intense concentration. Some of them were shouting or crying; others just stood there, frozen in place.\n\n'What's going on?' I asked again.\n\n'The Snow Crash virus,' the cabdriver said, shaking his head. 'It's like nothing anyone's ever seen before. People are getting infected left and right, and when they do, they start seeing this virtual reality world that's so real it's like a dream come true. But it's not just a dream - it's a trap. Once you're in, there's no way out.'\n\nI felt a cold sweat break out on my forehead as I realized what was happening. The Snow Crash virus was spreading like wildfire through the streets of New York, infecting people left and right. And once someone got infected, they were trapped in this virtual world, unable to return to reality.\n\nThe cabdriver pulled up to the curb and turned off the meter. 'You're going to have to get out here,' he said. 'I'm not going any further into that mess.'\n\nI looked around at the chaos on the streets and knew that I had to get out of there, too. But where could I go? The Snow Crash virus was everywhere, and once it got its hooks into you, there was no escape.\n\nI took a deep breath and stepped out onto the sidewalk, feeling the cool night air wash over me. The streets were still jammed with people, but at least they weren't getting any worse. Yet.\n\nI looked around for someplace to go, someplace that might be safe from the virus. And then I saw it: a small, unassuming building with a sign that read 'Hiro Protagonist's Pizza Palace'. It was the only place in sight that didn't seem to be affected by the chaos on the streets.\n\nI took off towards", "model": "llama3-8b", "seed": 2652832316, "prompt": "Please provide a long passage from \"Snow Crash\" by Neal Stephenson."}
